  {
    "id": "q_la_ATP_2026_9830",
    "fuente": "ATP",
    "capitulo": 2,
    "capituloTitulo": "Equipment, Navigation, and Facilities",
    "seccion": "Electronic Flight Instruments",
    "materia": "operaciones",
    "text": "Automation has been found to",
    "options": [
      "create higher workloads in terminal areas.",
      "improve crew situational awareness skills.",
      "substitute for a lack of aviation experience."
    ],
    "correctIndex": 1,
    "explanation": "Advanced avionics are designed to increase safety as well as the utility of the aircraft. Safety is enhanced by enabling better situational awareness. (PLT104, AA.I.A.R3) — FAA-H-8083-6",
    "cite": "ASA Airline Transport Pilot Test Prep 2025–2026 · Chapter 2 Equipment, Navigation, and Facilities · p. 2-11 · question 9830 · ALL",
    "sourceQuestionId": "9830",
    "sourceEdition": "2025–2026",
    "sourcePage": 11,
    "sourceCategories": [
      "ALL"
    ],
    "status": "publicada",
    "source": "import",
    "imagenes": []
  },
  {
    "id": "q_la_ATP_2026_9853",
    "fuente": "ATP",
    "capitulo": 2,
    "capituloTitulo": "Equipment, Navigation, and Facilities",
    "seccion": "Electronic Flight Instruments",
    "materia": "operaciones",
    "text": "When a pilot believes advanced avionics enable operations closer to personal or environmental limits,",
    "options": [
      "greater utilization of the aircraft is achieved.",
      "risk is increased.",
      "risk is decreased."
    ],
    "correctIndex": 1,
    "explanation": "Advanced avionics can sometimes have a negative effect on pilot risk-taking behavior, where more information results in pilots taking more risk than they might be willing to accept without the information. Advanced avionics should be used to increase safety, not risk. (PLT104, AA.I.A.R3) — FAA-H-8083-6",
    "cite": "ASA Airline Transport Pilot Test Prep 2025–2026 · Chapter 2 Equipment, Navigation, and Facilities · p. 2-11 · question 9853 · ALL",
    "sourceQuestionId": "9853",
    "sourceEdition": "2025–2026",
    "sourcePage": 11,
    "sourceCategories": [
      "ALL"
    ],
    "status": "publicada",
    "source": "import",
    "imagenes": []
  },
  {
    "id": "q_la_ATP_2026_9853_1",
    "fuente": "ATP",
    "capitulo": 2,
    "capituloTitulo": "Equipment, Navigation, and Facilities",
    "seccion": "Electronic Flight Instruments",
    "materia": "operaciones",
    "text": "In advanced avionics aircraft, proper automation management requires",
    "options": [
      "relying on flight management systems to navigate in order for the pilot to perform other tasks.",
      "a thorough understanding of how the autopilot interacts with other systems.",
      "the pilot to refrain from monitoring the automation after initial programming."
    ],
    "correctIndex": 1,
    "explanation": "Advanced avionics offer multiple levels of automation, from strictly manual flight to highly automated flight. No one level of automation is appropriate for all flight situations, but in order to avoid potentially dangerous distractions when flying with advanced avionics, the pilot must know how to manage the CDI, the navigation source, and the autopilot. It is important for a pilot to know the peculiarities of the particular automated system being used. (PLT104, AA.I.F.K3) — FAA-H-8083-25",
    "cite": "ASA Airline Transport Pilot Test Prep 2025–2026 · Chapter 2 Equipment, Navigation, and Facilities · p. 2-11 · question 9853-1 · ALL",
    "sourceQuestionId": "9853-1",
    "sourceEdition": "2025–2026",
    "sourcePage": 11,
    "sourceCategories": [
      "ALL"
    ],
    "status": "publicada",
    "source": "import",
    "imagenes": []
  },
  {
    "id": "q_la_ATP_2026_9854",
    "fuente": "ATP",
    "capitulo": 2,
    "capituloTitulo": "Equipment, Navigation, and Facilities",
    "seccion": "Electronic Flight Instruments",
    "materia": "operaciones",
    "text": "Automation in aircraft has proven",
    "options": [
      "to present new hazards in its limitations.",
      "that automation is basically flawless.",
      "effective in preventing accidents."
    ],
    "correctIndex": 0,
    "explanation": "Advanced avionics are designed to increase safety as well as the utility of the aircraft. However, the systems are not infallible. While automation does help prevent many existing types of errors, it has also created new kinds of errors. (PLT104, AA.I.A.R3) — FAA-H-8083-6",
    "cite": "ASA Airline Transport Pilot Test Prep 2025–2026 · Chapter 2 Equipment, Navigation, and Facilities · p. 2-11 · question 9854 · ALL",
    "sourceQuestionId": "9854",
    "sourceEdition": "2025–2026",
    "sourcePage": 11,
    "sourceCategories": [
      "ALL"
    ],
    "status": "publicada",
    "source": "import",
    "imagenes": []
  },
  {
    "id": "q_la_ATP_2026_9855",
    "fuente": "ATP",
    "capitulo": 2,
    "capituloTitulo": "Equipment, Navigation, and Facilities",
    "seccion": "Electronic Flight Instruments",
    "materia": "operaciones",
    "text": "The lighter workloads associated with glass (digital) flight instrumentation",
    "options": [
      "are useful in decreasing flightcrew fatigue.",
      "have proven to increase safety in operations.",
      "may lead to complacency by the flightcrew."
    ],
    "correctIndex": 2,
    "explanation": "Risk management is the last of the three flight management skills needed for mastery of the advanced avionics aircraft. The enhanced situational awareness and automation capabilities offered by a glass flight deck vastly expand its safety and utility, especially for personal transportation use. At the same time, there is some risk that lighter workloads could lead to complacency. (PLT104, AA.I.A.R3) — FAA-H-8083-2",
    "cite": "ASA Airline Transport Pilot Test Prep 2025–2026 · Chapter 2 Equipment, Navigation, and Facilities · p. 2-11 · question 9855 · ALL",
    "sourceQuestionId": "9855",
    "sourceEdition": "2025–2026",
    "sourcePage": 11,
    "sourceCategories": [
      "ALL"
    ],
    "status": "publicada",
    "source": "import",
    "imagenes": []
  },
  {
    "id": "q_la_ATP_2026_9857",
    "fuente": "ATP",
    "capitulo": 2,
    "capituloTitulo": "Equipment, Navigation, and Facilities",
    "seccion": "Electronic Flight Instruments",
    "materia": "operaciones",
    "text": "Humans are characteristically",
    "options": [
      "disposed to appreciate the workload imposed by automation.",
      "disposed to expect automation to fail often.",
      "poor monitors of automated systems."
    ],
    "correctIndex": 2,
    "explanation": "Humans are characteristically poor monitors of automated systems. When passively monitoring an automated system for faults, abnormalities, or other infrequent events, humans perform poorly. The more reliable the system is, the worse the human performance becomes. For example, the pilot monitors only a backup alert system, rather than the situation that the alert system is designed to safeguard. It is a paradox of automation that technically advanced avionics can both increase and decrease pilot awareness. (PLT104, AA.I.A.R3) — FAA-H-8083-2",
    "cite": "ASA Airline Transport Pilot Test Prep 2025–2026 · Chapter 2 Equipment, Navigation, and Facilities · p. 2-12 · question 9857 · ALL",
    "sourceQuestionId": "9857",
    "sourceEdition": "2025–2026",
    "sourcePage": 12,
    "sourceCategories": [
      "ALL"
    ],
    "status": "publicada",
    "source": "import",
    "imagenes": []
  },
  {
    "id": "q_la_ATP_2026_8711",
    "fuente": "ATP",
    "capitulo": 2,
    "capituloTitulo": "Equipment, Navigation, and Facilities",
    "seccion": "Electronic Flight Instruments",
    "materia": "operaciones",
    "text": "Reliance on automation can translate to",
    "options": [
      "decreased flight deck workload.",
      "increased error awareness.",
      "lack of manual handling skills."
    ],
    "correctIndex": 2,
    "explanation": "A reliance on automation translates into a lack of basic flying skills that may affect the pilot’s ability to cope with an inflight emergency, such as a sudden mechanical failure. (PLT104, AA.I.A.R3) — FAA-8083-25",
    "cite": "ASA Airline Transport Pilot Test Prep 2025–2026 · Chapter 2 Equipment, Navigation, and Facilities · p. 2-12 · question 8711 · ALL",
    "sourceQuestionId": "8711",
    "sourceEdition": "2025–2026",
    "sourcePage": 12,
    "sourceCategories": [
      "ALL"
    ],
    "status": "publicada",
    "source": "import",
    "imagenes": []
  },
  {
    "id": "q_la_ATP_2026_9941",
    "fuente": "ATP",
    "capitulo": 2,
    "capituloTitulo": "Equipment, Navigation, and Facilities",
    "seccion": "Electronic Flight Instruments",
    "materia": "operaciones",
    "text": "Risk is increased when flightcrew members",
    "options": [
      "fail to monitor automated navigation systems.",
      "allocate time to verify expected performance of automated systems.",
      "question the performance of each other’s duties."
    ],
    "correctIndex": 0,
    "explanation": "Flightcrew members must stay vigilant and actively involved throughout the flight, and avoid being complacent and overly dependent on the automated navigation systems. (PLT047, AA.I.A.R3) — FAA-H-8083-2",
    "cite": "ASA Airline Transport Pilot Test Prep 2025–2026 · Chapter 2 Equipment, Navigation, and Facilities · p. 2-12 · question 9941 · ALL",
    "sourceQuestionId": "9941",
    "sourceEdition": "2025–2026",
    "sourcePage": 12,
    "sourceCategories": [
      "ALL"
    ],
    "status": "publicada",
    "source": "import",
    "imagenes": []
  },
  {
    "id": "q_la_ATP_2026_9410",
    "fuente": "ATP",
    "capitulo": 2,
    "capituloTitulo": "Equipment, Navigation, and Facilities",
    "seccion": "Safety of Flight Equipment",
    "materia": "operaciones",
    "text": "Information obtained from flight data and flight deck voice recorders shall be used only for determining",
    "options": [
      "who was responsible for any accident or incident.",
      "evidence for use in civil penalty or certificate action.",
      "possible causes of accidents or incidents."
    ],
    "correctIndex": 2,
    "explanation": "Information obtained from flight data and flight deck voice recorders is used to assist in determining the cause of accidents or occurrences in connection with investigation under NTSB Part 830. The Administrator does not use the flight deck voice recorder record in any civil penalty or certificate action. (PLT388, AA.I.G.K2) — 14 CFR §91.609 Answer (A) is incorrect because flight data or flight deck voice recorders are only used to determine possible causes of accidents or incidents. Answer (B) is incorrect because flight data or flight deck voice recorders may not be used for any civil penalty or certificate action.",
    "cite": "ASA Airline Transport Pilot Test Prep 2025–2026 · Chapter 2 Equipment, Navigation, and Facilities · p. 2-13 · question 9410 · ALL",
    "sourceQuestionId": "9410",
    "sourceEdition": "2025–2026",
    "sourcePage": 13,
    "sourceCategories": [
      "ALL"
    ],
    "status": "publicada",
    "source": "import",
    "imagenes": []
  },
  {
    "id": "q_la_ATP_2026_9356",
    "fuente": "ATP",
    "capitulo": 2,
    "capituloTitulo": "Equipment, Navigation, and Facilities",
    "seccion": "Safety of Flight Equipment",
    "materia": "operaciones",
    "text": "For what purpose may flight deck voice recorders and flight data recorders NOT be used?",
    "options": [
      "Determining causes of accidents and occurrences under investigation by the NTSB.",
      "Determining any certificate action, or civil penalty, arising out of an accident or occurrence.",
      "Identifying procedures that may have been conducive to any accident, or occurrence resulting in investigation under NTSB Part 830."
    ],
    "correctIndex": 1,
    "explanation": "Information obtained from flight data and flight deck voice recorders is used to assist in determining the cause of accidents or occurrences in connection with investigation under NTSB Part 830. The Administrator does not use the flight deck voice recorder record in any civil penalty or certificate action. (PLT388, AA.I.G.K2) — 14 CFR §91.609 Answer (A) is incorrect because flight deck voice recorders and flight data recorders are used to determine causes of accidents or occurrences. Answer (C) is incorrect because flight data recorders and flight deck voice recorders are used to identify any procedures, malfunction, or failure that may have contributed to an accident or occurrence.",
    "cite": "ASA Airline Transport Pilot Test Prep 2025–2026 · Chapter 2 Equipment, Navigation, and Facilities · p. 2-13 · question 9356 · ALL",
    "sourceQuestionId": "9356",
    "sourceEdition": "2025–2026",
    "sourcePage": 13,
    "sourceCategories": [
