  {
    "id": "q_la_ATP_2026_8965",
    "fuente": "ATP",
    "capitulo": 2,
    "capituloTitulo": "Equipment, Navigation, and Facilities",
    "seccion": "Approach Systems",
    "materia": "operaciones",
    "text": "How does the SDF differ from an ILS LOC?",
    "options": [
      "SDF – 6° or 12° wide, ILS – 3° to 6°.",
      "SDF – offset from runway plus 4°, ILS – aligned with runway.",
      "SDF – 15° usable off course indications, ILS – 35°."
    ],
    "correctIndex": 0,
    "explanation": "The SDF signal is fixed at either 6° or 12° as necessary to provide maximum flyability and optimum course quality. (PLT356, AA.VI.E.K3) — AIM ¶1-1-10 Answer (B) is incorrect because an SDF may or may not be aligned with the centerline of the runway. Answer (C) is incorrect because the usable off course indications are limited to 35° for both types of approaches.",
    "cite": "ASA Airline Transport Pilot Test Prep 2025–2026 · Chapter 2 Equipment, Navigation, and Facilities · p. 2-35 · question 8965 · ATM, ATS, RTC",
