  {
    "id": "q_la_ATP_2026_9723",
    "fuente": "ATP",
    "capitulo": 2,
    "capituloTitulo": "Equipment, Navigation, and Facilities",
    "seccion": "Global Positioning System (GPS)",
    "materia": "operaciones",
    "text": "Authorization to conduct any GPS operation under IFR requires that",
    "options": [
      "the equipment be approved in accordance with TSO C-115a.",
      "the pilot review appropriate weather, aircraft flight manual (AFM), and operation of the particular GPS receiver.",
      "procedures must be established for use in the event that the loss of RAIM capability is predicted to occur."
    ],
    "correctIndex": 2,
    "explanation": "The GPS receiver verifies the integrity (usability) of the signals received from the GPS constellation through receiver autonomous integrity monitoring (RAIM) to determine if a satellite is providing corrupted information. At least one satellite, in addition to those required for navigation, must be in view for the receiver to perform the RAIM function. In the event of loss of RAIM, the flight must rely on other approved equipment, the departure must be delayed, or the flight canceled. (PLT354, AA.VI.D.K2) — AIM ¶1-1-17 Answer (A) is incorrect because the equipment must be approved in accordance with TSO C-129, not TSO C-115a. Answer (B) is incorrect because while the pilot is responsible for reviewing the weather before any flight, this requirement is not specific to GPS operations.",
