  {
    "id": "q_la_ATP_2026_8905",
    "fuente": "ATP",
    "capitulo": 2,
    "capituloTitulo": "Equipment, Navigation, and Facilities",
    "seccion": "Airport Lighting and Marking",
    "materia": "operaciones",
    "text": "How can a pilot identify a military airport at night?",
    "options": [
      "Green, yellow, and white beacon light.",
      "White and red beacon light with dual flash of the white.",
      "Green and white beacon light with dual flash of the white."
    ],
    "correctIndex": 2,
    "explanation": "Military airport beacons flash alternately white and green, but are differentiated from civil beacons by a dual peaked (two quick) white flashes between the green flashes. (PLT141, AA.II.C.K3) — AIM ¶2-1-9 Answer (A) is incorrect because a sequential green, yellow, and white beacon light identifies a lighted civilian heliport. Answer (B) is incorrect because no type of airfield is marked by a beacon with a red and white light with dual flash of the white.",
    "cite": "ASA Airline Transport Pilot Test Prep 2025–2026 · Chapter 2 Equipment, Navigation, and Facilities · p. 2-44 · question 8905 · ATM, ATS, RTC",
