      "atp_2026_figure-223-unit.svg"
    ]
  },
  {
    "id": "q_la_ATP_2026_9735_1",
    "fuente": "ATP",
    "capitulo": 2,
    "capituloTitulo": "Equipment, Navigation, and Facilities",
    "seccion": "Airport Lighting and Marking",
    "materia": "operaciones",
    "text": "(Refer to Figure 228.) What is the purpose of the runway/runway hold position sign?",
    "options": [
      "Denotes entrance to runway from a taxiway.",
      "Denotes area protected for an aircraft approaching or departing a runway.",
      "Denotes intersecting runways."
    ],
    "correctIndex": 2,
    "explanation": "Mandatory instruction signs are used to denote an entrance to a runway or critical area and areas where an aircraft is prohibited from entering. The runway hold position sign is located at the holding position on taxiways that intersect a runway or on runways that intersect other runways. (PLT141, AA.II.C.K3) — AIM ¶2-3-8",
    "cite": "ASA Airline Transport Pilot Test Prep 2025–2026 · Chapter 2 Equipment, Navigation, and Facilities · p. 2-45 · question 9735-1 · ATM, ATS, RTC",
    "sourceQuestionId": "9735-1",
    "sourceEdition": "2025–2026",
    "sourcePage": 45,
    "sourceCategories": [
      "ATM",
      "ATS",
      "RTC"
    ],
    "status": "publicada",
    "source": "import",
    "imagenes": [
      "atp_2026_figure-228-unit.svg"
    ]
  },
  {
    "id": "q_la_ATP_2026_9735_2",
    "fuente": "ATP",
    "capitulo": 2,
    "capituloTitulo": "Equipment, Navigation, and Facilities",
    "seccion": "Airport Lighting and Marking",
    "materia": "operaciones",
    "text": "(Refer to Figure 225.) What is the purpose of no entry sign?",
    "options": [
      "Identifies paved area where aircraft are prohibited from entering.",
      "Identifies area that does not continue beyond intersection.",
      "Identifies the exit boundary for the runway protected area."
    ],
    "correctIndex": 0,
    "explanation": "The no entry sign prohibits an aircraft from entering an area. Typically, this sign would be located on a taxiway intended to be used in only one direction or at the intersection of vehicle roadways with runways, taxiways, or aprons where the roadway may be mistaken as a taxiway or other aircraft movement surface. (PLT141, AA.II.C.K3) — AIM ¶2-3-8 Answer (B) is incorrect because this is the purpose of a hold position sign. Answer (C) is incorrect because this is the purpose of the runway boundary sign.",
    "cite": "ASA Airline Transport Pilot Test Prep 2025–2026 · Chapter 2 Equipment, Navigation, and Facilities · p. 2-45 · question 9735-2 · ATM, ATS, RTC",
    "sourceQuestionId": "9735-2",
    "sourceEdition": "2025–2026",
    "sourcePage": 45,
    "sourceCategories": [
      "ATM",
      "ATS",
      "RTC"
    ],
    "status": "publicada",
    "source": "import",
    "imagenes": [
      "atp_2026_figure-225-unit.svg"
    ]
  },
  {
    "id": "q_la_ATP_2026_9735_3",
    "fuente": "ATP",
    "capitulo": 2,
    "capituloTitulo": "Equipment, Navigation, and Facilities",
    "seccion": "Airport Lighting and Marking",
    "materia": "operaciones",
    "text": "(Refer to Figure 226.) What does the outbound destination sign identify?",
    "options": [
      "Identifies entrance to the runway from a taxiway.",
      "Identifies runway on which an aircraft is located.",
      "Identifies direction to take-off runways."
    ],
    "correctIndex": 2,
    "explanation": "Outbound destination signs define directions to takeoff runways. (PLT141, AA.II.C.K3) — AIM ¶2-3-11 Answer (A) is incorrect because this is a runway marking. Answer (B) is incorrect because this is a runway location sign.",
    "cite": "ASA Airline Transport Pilot Test Prep 2025–2026 · Chapter 2 Equipment, Navigation, and Facilities · p. 2-45 · question 9735-3 · ATM, ATS, RTC",
    "sourceQuestionId": "9735-3",
