  {
    "id": "q_la_ATP_2026_8914",
    "fuente": "ATP",
    "capitulo": 2,
    "capituloTitulo": "Equipment, Navigation, and Facilities",
    "seccion": "Airport Lighting and Marking",
    "materia": "operaciones",
    "text": "What is the purpose of REIL?",
    "options": [
      "Identification of a runway surrounded by a preponderance of other lighting.",
      "Identification of the touchdown zone to prevent landing short.",
      "Establish visual descent guidance information during an approach."
    ],
    "correctIndex": 0,
    "explanation": "Runway end identifier lights (REIL) are effective for: 1. Identification of a runway surrounded by numerous other lighting systems, 2. Identification of a runway which lacks contrast with surrounding terrain, or 3. Identification of a runway during reduced visibility. (PLT145, AA.III.A.K4) — AIM ¶2-1-3 Answer (B) is incorrect because the touchdown zone is identified by in-runway lighting of two rows of transverse light bars on either side of the runway centerline from 100 feet to 3,000 feet from the landing threshold. Answer (C) is incorrect because a VASI (not REIL) assists in providing visual descent guidance information during an approach.",
    "cite": "ASA Airline Transport Pilot Test Prep 2025–2026 · Chapter 2 Equipment, Navigation, and Facilities · p. 2-48 · question 8914 · ATM, ATS, RTC",
    "sourceQuestionId": "8914",
    "sourceEdition": "2025–2026",
    "sourcePage": 48,
    "sourceCategories": [
      "ATM",
      "ATS",
      "RTC"
    ],
    "status": "publicada",
    "source": "import",
    "imagenes": []
  },
  {
