    "fuente": "ATP",
    "capitulo": 2,
    "capituloTitulo": "Equipment, Navigation, and Facilities",
    "seccion": "Airport Lighting and Marking",
    "materia": "operaciones",
    "text": "Identify REIL.",
    "options": [
      "Amber lights for the first 2,000 feet of runway.",
      "Green lights at the threshold and red lights at far end of runway.",
      "Synchronized flashing lights laterally at each side of the runway threshold."
    ],
    "correctIndex": 2,
    "explanation": "The REIL system consists of a pair of synchronized flashing lights located laterally on each side of the runway threshold. (PLT145, AA.III.A.K4) — AIM ¶2-1-3 Answer (A) is incorrect because amber lights are used on the last 2,000 feet of runway edge lights to form a caution zone on instrument runways. Answer (B) is incorrect because green lights at the threshold mark the runway edge for landing aircraft and red lights at the far end mark the runway edge to a departing or landing aircraft.",
    "cite": "ASA Airline Transport Pilot Test Prep 2025–2026 · Chapter 2 Equipment, Navigation, and Facilities · p. 2-49 · question 8915 · ATM, ATS, RTC",
