  {
    "id": "q_la_ATP_2026_9436",
    "fuente": "ATP",
    "capitulo": 2,
    "capituloTitulo": "Equipment, Navigation, and Facilities",
    "seccion": "Airport Lighting and Marking",
    "materia": "operaciones",
    "text": "(Refer to Figure 156.) This sign, which is visible to the pilot on the runway, indicates",
    "options": [
      "a point at which the pilot should contact ground control without being instructed by the tower.",
      "a point at which the aircraft will be clear of the runway.",
      "the point at which the emergency arresting gear is stretched across the runway."
    ],
    "correctIndex": 1,
    "explanation": "The runway boundary sign has a yellow background with a black inscription with a graphic depicting the pavement holding position. This sign, which faces the runway and is visible to the pilot exiting the runway, is located adjacent to the holding position marking on the pavement. The sign is intended to provide pilots with another visual cue which they can use as a guide in deciding when they are “clear of the runway.” (PLT141, AA.I.E.K4) — AIM ¶2-3-9",
    "cite": "ASA Airline Transport Pilot Test Prep 2025–2026 · Chapter 2 Equipment, Navigation, and Facilities · p. 2-50 · question 9436 · ATM, ATS, RTC",
