  {
    "id": "q_la_ATP_2026_8361",
    "fuente": "ATP",
    "capitulo": 3,
    "capituloTitulo": "Aerodynamics",
    "seccion": "Critical Engine and VMC",
    "materia": "operaciones",
    "text": "Which engine is the “critical” engine of a twinengine airplane?",
    "options": [
      "The one with the center of thrust closest to the centerline of the fuselage.",
      "The one designated by the manufacturer which develops most usable thrust.",
      "The one with the center of thrust farthest from the centerline of the fuselage."
    ],
    "correctIndex": 0,
    "explanation": "The critical engine is defined as the engine whose failure would most adversely affect performance or handling. Because of P-factor, most propeller-driven airplanes do not develop symmetrical thrust at high angles of attack. If the engine with the thrust line closest to the airplane centerline fails, the resulting yawing moment will be greater than if the other engine had failed. (PLT347, AA.VII.B.K2) — FAA-H-8083-3 V for",
    "cite": "ASA Airline Transport Pilot Test Prep 2025–2026 · Chapter 3 Aerodynamics · p. 3-9 · question 8361 · ATM, ADX",
