  {
    "id": "q_la_ATP_2026_8389_1",
    "fuente": "ATP",
    "capitulo": 3,
    "capituloTitulo": "Aerodynamics",
    "seccion": "High Speed Flight",
    "materia": "operaciones",
    "text": "When piloting a turbojet transport airplane, what is a possible result when operating at speeds 5 to 10 percent above the critical Mach number?",
    "options": [
      "Increased aerodynamic efficiency.",
      "Decreased control surface effectiveness.",
      "Occasional low speed Mach buffet warnings."
    ],
    "correctIndex": 1,
    "explanation": "Critical Mach number is the boundary between subsonic and transonic flight. When shock waves form on the aircraft, airflow separation followed by buffet and aircraft control difficulties can occur. Shock waves, buffet, and airflow separation take place above critical Mach number. (PLT214, AA.I.B.K4) — FAA-H-8083-25",
    "cite": "ASA Airline Transport Pilot Test Prep 2025–2026 · Chapter 3 Aerodynamics · p. 3-16 · question 8389-1 · ATM, ATS, ADX",
    "sourceQuestionId": "8389-1",
    "sourceEdition": "2025–2026",
    "sourcePage": 16,
    "sourceCategories": [
      "ATM",
      "ATS",
      "ADX"
    ],
    "status": "publicada",
    "source": "import",
    "imagenes": []
  },
  {
    "id": "q_la_ATP_2026_8392",
    "fuente": "ATP",
    "capitulo": 3,
    "capituloTitulo": "Aerodynamics",
    "seccion": "High Speed Flight",
    "materia": "operaciones",
    "text": "What is the result of a shock-induced separation of airflow occurring symmetrically near the wing root of a sweptwing aircraft?",
    "options": [
      "A high-speed stall and sudden pitchup.",
      "A severe moment or “Mach tuck.”",
      "Severe porpoising."
    ],
    "correctIndex": 1,
    "explanation": "If the shock-induced separation occurs symmetrically near the wing root, there is an accompanying loss of lift. A decrease in downwash on the horizontal tail will create a diving moment and the aircraft will “tuck under.” (PLT214, AA.I.D.K1) — ANA Answer (A) is incorrect because there is a sudden pitch down when a shock-induced separation of airflow occurs symmetrically near the wing root of a sweptwing aircraft. Answer (C) is incorrect because there is a diving moment when a shock-induced separation of airflow occurs symmetrically near the wing root of a sweptwing aircraft.",
    "cite": "ASA Airline Transport Pilot Test Prep 2025–2026 · Chapter 3 Aerodynamics · p. 3-17 · question 8392 · ATM, ATS, ADX",
    "sourceQuestionId": "8392",
    "sourceEdition": "2025–2026",
    "sourcePage": 17,
    "sourceCategories": [
      "ATM",
      "ATS",
      "ADX"
    ],
    "status": "publicada",
    "source": "import",
    "imagenes": []
  },
  {
    "id": "q_la_ATP_2026_8395",
    "fuente": "ATP",
    "capitulo": 3,
    "capituloTitulo": "Aerodynamics",
    "seccion": "High Speed Flight",
    "materia": "operaciones",
    "text": "What is the movement of the center of pressure when the wingtips of a sweptwing airplane are shockstalled first?",
    "options": [
      "Inward and aft.",
      "Inward and forward.",
      "Outward and forward."
    ],
    "correctIndex": 1,
    "explanation": "Shock formation at the wing tip first moves the center of pressure forward and inboard and the resulting climbing moment and tail downwash can contribute to “pitch up.” (PLT214, AA.I.D.K1) — ANA Answer (A) is incorrect because when the wing tips are shock-stalled first, the center of pressure moves forward. Answer (C) is incorrect because when the wing tips are shock-stalled first, the center of pressure moves inward.",
    "cite": "ASA Airline Transport Pilot Test Prep 2025–2026 · Chapter 3 Aerodynamics · p. 3-17 · question 8395 · ATM, ATS, ADX",
    "sourceQuestionId": "8395",
    "sourceEdition": "2025–2026",
    "sourcePage": 17,
    "sourceCategories": [
      "ATM",
      "ATS",
      "ADX"
    ],
    "status": "publicada",
    "source": "import",
    "imagenes": []
  },
  {
    "id": "q_la_ATP_2026_8391",
    "fuente": "ATP",
    "capitulo": 3,
    "capituloTitulo": "Aerodynamics",
    "seccion": "High Speed Flight",
    "materia": "operaciones",
    "text": "What is the principal advantage of a sweepback design wing over a straightwing design?",
    "options": [
      "The critical Mach number will increase significantly.",
      "Sweepback will increase changes in the magnitude of force coefficients due to compressibility.",
      "Sweepback will accelerate the onset of compressibility effect."
    ],
    "correctIndex": 0,
    "explanation": "One of the most important advantages of sweep is an increase in critical Mach number, force divergence Mach number, and the Mach number at which the drag rise will peak. In other words, the sweep will delay the onset of compressibility effects. (PLT214, AA.I.D.K1) — ANA",
    "cite": "ASA Airline Transport Pilot Test Prep 2025–2026 · Chapter 3 Aerodynamics · p. 3-17 · question 8391 · ATM, ATS, ADX",
    "sourceQuestionId": "8391",
    "sourceEdition": "2025–2026",
    "sourcePage": 17,
    "sourceCategories": [
      "ATM",
      "ATS",
      "ADX"
    ],
    "status": "publicada",
    "source": "import",
    "imagenes": []
  },
  {
    "id": "q_la_ATP_2026_8391_1",
    "fuente": "ATP",
    "capitulo": 3,
    "capituloTitulo": "Aerodynamics",
    "seccion": "High Speed Flight",
    "materia": "operaciones",
    "text": "Swept wings",
    "options": [
      "improve specific fuel consumption.",
      "increase the critical Mach number.",
      "increase the speed of sound quotient."
    ],
    "correctIndex": 1,
    "explanation": "One of the advantages of sweep in wing design is the increase in the critical Mach number it creates. The increase in critical Mach number, force divergence Mach number, and the Mach number at which the drag-rise peaks will delay the onset of compressibility effects. (PLT094, AA.I.D.K1) — ANA",
    "cite": "ASA Airline Transport Pilot Test Prep 2025–2026 · Chapter 3 Aerodynamics · p. 3-17 · question 8391-1 · ATM, ATS, ADX",
    "sourceQuestionId": "8391-1",
    "sourceEdition": "2025–2026",
    "sourcePage": 17,
    "sourceCategories": [
      "ATM",
      "ATS",
      "ADX"
    ],
    "status": "publicada",
    "source": "import",
    "imagenes": []
  },
  {
    "id": "q_la_ATP_2026_8391_2",
    "fuente": "ATP",
    "capitulo": 3,
    "capituloTitulo": "Aerodynamics",
    "seccion": "High Speed Flight",
    "materia": "operaciones",
    "text": "For significant benefit, wing sweep must be at least",
    "options": [
      "30° to 35°.",
      "45° to 50°.",
      "55° or more to substantially delay compressibility effects."
    ],
    "correctIndex": 0,
    "explanation": "If sweepback is to be used at all, at least 30° to 35° must be used to produce any significant benefit. (PLT094, AA.I.D.K1) — ANA",
    "cite": "ASA Airline Transport Pilot Test Prep 2025–2026 · Chapter 3 Aerodynamics · p. 3-17 · question 8391-2 · ATM, ATS, ADX",
    "sourceQuestionId": "8391-2",
    "sourceEdition": "2025–2026",
