  {
    "id": "q_la_ATP_2026_8394_2",
    "fuente": "ATP",
    "capitulo": 3,
    "capituloTitulo": "Aerodynamics",
    "seccion": "High Speed Flight",
    "materia": "operaciones",
    "text": "A turbojet airplane has an increase in specific range with altitude, which can be attributed to three factors. One of those factors is",
    "options": [
      "an increase in altitude in the troposphere results in higher energy air flow.",
      "an increase in proportion of velocity versus thrust required.",
      "decreased engine turbine speeds."
    ],
    "correctIndex": 1,
    "explanation": "One of the three factors that leads to the increase in specific range with altitude in a turbojet airplane is that higher altitude increases the proportion of velocity versus required thrust, which in turn provides a greater true airspeed for the same amount of thrust. The other two factors involved are that (1) an increase in tropospheric altitude produces a lower inlet air temperature, which then reduces the specific fuel consumption; and (2) an increase in altitude requires increased engine RPM for cruise thrust, and then the specific fuel consumption is reduced as the engine approaches the normal-rated RPM. (PLT094, AA.I.D.K1) — ANA ATM, ATS, ADX.",
    "cite": "ASA Airline Transport Pilot Test Prep 2025–2026 · Chapter 3 Aerodynamics · p. 3-18 · question 8394-2 · ATM, ATS, ADX",
    "sourceQuestionId": "8394-2",
    "sourceEdition": "2025–2026",
    "sourcePage": 18,
    "sourceCategories": [
      "ATM",
      "ATS",
      "ADX"
    ],
    "status": "publicada",
    "source": "import",
    "imagenes": []
  },
  {
    "id": "q_la_ATP_2026_8394_3",
    "fuente": "ATP",
    "capitulo": 3,
    "capituloTitulo": "Aerodynamics",
    "seccion": "High Speed Flight",
    "materia": "operaciones",
    "text": "While operating a turbojet transport airplane at high altitude, which of the following is most likely to cause a low speed Mach buffet?",
    "options": [
      "Reducing the angle of attack after a high speed Mach buffet.",
      "The airplane is flown too fast for its weight and altitude.",
      "The airplane is flown too slow for its weight and altitude."
    ],
    "correctIndex": 2,
    "explanation": "Mach buffet is a function of the speed of the airflow over the wing—not necessarily the speed of the aircraft. An aircraft flown at a speed too slow for its weight and altitude necessitating a high angle of attack is the most likely situation to cause a low speed Mach buffet. (PLT094, AA.I.D.K9) — FAA-H-8083-25",
    "cite": "ASA Airline Transport Pilot Test Prep 2025–2026 · Chapter 3 Aerodynamics · p. 3-18 · question 8394-3 · ATM, ATS, ADX",
    "sourceQuestionId": "8394-3",
    "sourceEdition": "2025–2026",
    "sourcePage": 18,
    "sourceCategories": [
      "ATM",
      "ATS",
      "ADX"
    ],
    "status": "publicada",
    "source": "import",
    "imagenes": []
  },
  {
    "id": "q_la_ATP_2026_8326",
    "fuente": "ATP",
    "capitulo": 3,
    "capituloTitulo": "Aerodynamics",
    "seccion": "Primary Flight Controls",
    "materia": "operaciones",
    "text": "Which of the following is considered a primary flight control?",
    "options": [
      "Slats.",
      "Elevator.",
      "Dorsal fin."
    ],
    "correctIndex": 1,
    "explanation": "The primary group of flight control surfaces consists of ailerons, elevators, and rudders. (PLT346, AA.I.A.K12) — FAA-H-8083-25 Answer (A) is incorrect because slats are high-lift devices, not a flight control device. Answer (C) is incorrect because a dorsal fin is not a primary flight control, but is used to provide directional stability.",
    "cite": "ASA Airline Transport Pilot Test Prep 2025–2026 · Chapter 3 Aerodynamics · p. 3-20 · question 8326 · ATM, ATS, ADX",
    "sourceQuestionId": "8326",
    "sourceEdition": "2025–2026",
