    "imagenes": []
  },
  {
    "id": "q_la_ATP_2026_8327",
    "fuente": "ATP",
    "capitulo": 3,
    "capituloTitulo": "Aerodynamics",
    "seccion": "Primary Flight Controls",
    "materia": "operaciones",
    "text": "Which of the following is considered an auxiliary flight control?",
    "options": [
      "Ruddervator.",
      "Upper rudder.",
      "Leading-edge flaps."
    ],
    "correctIndex": 2,
    "explanation": "Auxiliary wing flight surfaces include trailing edge flaps, leading edge flaps, speed brakes, spoilers, and leading edge slats. (PLT473, AA.I.A.K12) — FAA-H-8083-25 Answer (A) is incorrect because a ruddervator is a primary flight control surface that incorporates both a rudder and elevator into one surface. Answer (B) is incorrect because upper rudders (found on the B-727) are stand-by rudders, which are used in the event of a hydraulic system failure.",
    "cite": "ASA Airline Transport Pilot Test Prep 2025–2026 · Chapter 3 Aerodynamics · p. 3-20 · question 8327 · ATM, ATS, ADX",
    "sourceQuestionId": "8327",
    "sourceEdition": "2025–2026",
