  {
    "id": "q_la_ATP_2026_8342",
    "fuente": "ATP",
    "capitulo": 3,
    "capituloTitulo": "Aerodynamics",
    "seccion": "Primary Flight Controls",
    "materia": "operaciones",
    "text": "Why do some airplanes equipped with inboard/ outboard ailerons use the outboards for slow flight only?",
    "options": [
      "Increased surface area provides greater controllability with flap extension.",
      "Aerodynamic loads on the outboard ailerons tend to twist the wingtips at high speeds.",
      "Locking out the outboard ailerons in high-speed flight provides variable flight control feel."
    ],
    "correctIndex": 1,
    "explanation": "Aerodynamic loads on the outboard ailerons tend to twist the wing tips at high speeds. This results in deformation great enough to nullify the effect of aileron deflection and create rolling moments opposite to the direction commanded. Because of this, outboard ailerons are used for slow flight only. (PLT346, AA.I.A.K12) — FAA-H-8083-25",
    "cite": "ASA Airline Transport Pilot Test Prep 2025–2026 · Chapter 3 Aerodynamics · p. 3-20 · question 8342 · ATM, ATS, ADX",
    "sourceQuestionId": "8342",
    "sourceEdition": "2025–2026",
