  {
    "id": "q_la_ATP_2026_8341",
    "fuente": "ATP",
    "capitulo": 3,
    "capituloTitulo": "Aerodynamics",
    "seccion": "Primary Flight Controls",
    "materia": "operaciones",
    "text": "Which is a purpose of wing-mounted vortex generators?",
    "options": [
      "Delays the onset of drag divergence at high speeds and aids in maintaining aileron effectiveness at high speeds.",
      "Increase the onset of drag divergence and aid in aileron effectiveness at low speeds.",
      "Breaks the airflow over the wing so the stall will progress from the root out to the tip of the wing."
    ],
    "correctIndex": 0,
    "explanation": "The function of the vortex generators is to create a strong vortex which introduces high velocity, high energy air next to the surface to reduce or delay the shock-induced separation. (PLT266, AA.I.A.K12) — FAA-H-8083-25 Answer (B) is incorrect because vortex generators are most effective at high speeds and the increased drag that they produce is not their primary function. Answer (C) is incorrect because a stall strip breaks the airflow over the wing so the stall will progress from the root out to the tip of the wing.",
    "cite": "ASA Airline Transport Pilot Test Prep 2025–2026 · Chapter 3 Aerodynamics · p. 3-22 · question 8341 · ATM, ATS, ADX",
    "sourceQuestionId": "8341",
    "sourceEdition": "2025–2026",
    "sourcePage": 22,
    "sourceCategories": [
      "ATM",
      "ATS",
      "ADX"
    ],
    "status": "publicada",
    "source": "import",
    "imagenes": []
  },
  {
    "id": "q_la_ATP_2026_8356",
    "fuente": "ATP",
    "capitulo": 3,
    "capituloTitulo": "Aerodynamics",
    "seccion": "Primary Flight Controls",
    "materia": "operaciones",
    "text": "Airflow separation over the wing can be delayed by using vortex generators",
    "options": [
      "directing high pressure air over the top of the wing or flap through slots and making the wing surface smooth.",
      "directing a suction over the top of the wing or flap through slots and making the wing surface smooth.",
      "making the wing surface rough and/or directing high pressure air over the top of the wing or flap through slots."
    ],
    "correctIndex": 2,
    "explanation": "Vortex generators prevent undesirable airflow separation from the surface by mixing the boundary airflow with the high energy airflow just above the surface. The vortex generators mix the turbulent outer layers of the boundary layers with the slow moving laminar lower layers thus reenergizing them. (PLT266, AA.I.A.K12) — FAA-H-8083-25",
    "cite": "ASA Airline Transport Pilot Test Prep 2025–2026 · Chapter 3 Aerodynamics · p. 3-22 · question 8356 · ATM, ATS, ADX",
    "sourceQuestionId": "8356",
    "sourceEdition": "2025–2026",
    "sourcePage": 22,
    "sourceCategories": [
      "ATM",
      "ATS",
      "ADX"
    ],
    "status": "publicada",
    "source": "import",
    "imagenes": []
  },
  {
    "id": "q_la_ATP_2026_9759",
    "fuente": "ATP",
    "capitulo": 3,
    "capituloTitulo": "Aerodynamics",
    "seccion": "Primary Flight Controls",
    "materia": "operaciones",
    "text": "If the boundary layer separates",
    "options": [
      "drag is decreased.",
      "the wing is about to stall and stop producing lift.",
      "ice will sublimate and not freeze."
    ],
    "correctIndex": 1,
    "explanation": "The boundary layer gives any object an effective shape that is usually slightly different from the physical shape. The boundary layer may also separate from the body, thus creating an effective shape much different from the physical shape of the object. This change in the physical shape of the boundary layer causes a dramatic decrease in lift and an increase in drag. When this happens, the airfoil has stalled. (PLT266, AA.V.A.K1) — FAA-H-8083-25",
    "cite": "ASA Airline Transport Pilot Test Prep 2025–2026 · Chapter 3 Aerodynamics · p. 3-22 · question 9759 · ALL",
    "sourceQuestionId": "9759",
    "sourceEdition": "2025–2026",
    "sourcePage": 22,
    "sourceCategories": [
      "ALL"
    ],
    "status": "publicada",
    "source": "import",
    "imagenes": []
  },
  {
    "id": "q_la_ATP_2026_8330",
    "fuente": "ATP",
    "capitulo": 3,
    "capituloTitulo": "Aerodynamics",
    "seccion": "Tabs",
    "materia": "operaciones",
    "text": "What is the purpose of a servo tab?",
    "options": [
      "Move the flight controls in the event of manual reversion.",
      "Reduce control forces by deflecting in the proper direction to move a primary flight control.",
      "Prevent a control surface from moving to a full-deflection position due to aerodynamic forces."
    ],
    "correctIndex": 1,
    "explanation": "The servo tab moves in response to the flight deck control. The force of the airflow on the servo tab then moves the primary control surface. (PLT473, AA.I.A.K12) — FAA-H-8083-25 Answer (A) is incorrect because, in the event of manual reversion on some transport category aircraft, the control tabs would move the flight controls. Answer (C) is incorrect because the purpose of the anti-servo tab is to preclude full deflection of control surfaces.",
    "cite": "ASA Airline Transport Pilot Test Prep 2025–2026 · Chapter 3 Aerodynamics · p. 3-23 · question 8330 · ATM, ATS, ADX",
    "sourceQuestionId": "8330",
    "sourceEdition": "2025–2026",
    "sourcePage": 23,
    "sourceCategories": [
