  {
    "id": "q_la_ATP_2026_9765",
    "fuente": "ATP",
    "capitulo": 3,
    "capituloTitulo": "Aerodynamics",
    "seccion": "High-Lift Devices",
    "materia": "operaciones",
    "text": "What is a difference between the fowler flap system and split flap system?",
    "options": [
      "Fowler flaps produce the greatest change in pitching moment.",
      "Fowler flaps produce more drag.",
      "Split flaps cause the greatest change in twisting loads."
    ],
    "correctIndex": 0,
    "explanation": "Fowler flaps slide out and downward from the trailing edge of the wing. When lowered, they increase the wing area as well as the wing camber. The Fowler flap is characterized by the largest increase in CL with the MAX least changes in drag. The Fowler flap also creates the greatest change in pitching moment. Split flaps consist of a hinged plate that deflects downward from the lower surface of the wing and produce the least change in the pitching moments of a wing when it is lowered. The deflection of a flap causes large nose-down moments which create significant twisting loads on the structure and pitching moments that must be controlled with the horizontal tail. Unfortunately, the flap types producing the greatest increases in CL usually cause the greatest MA X twisting moments. The Fowler flap causes the greatest change in twisting moment while the split flap causes the least. (PLT266, AA.I.A.K12) — ANA",
    "cite": "ASA Airline Transport Pilot Test Prep 2025–2026 · Chapter 3 Aerodynamics · p. 3-26 · question 9765 · ATM, ATS, ADX",
    "sourceQuestionId": "9765",
    "sourceEdition": "2025–2026",
    "sourcePage": 26,
    "sourceCategories": [
      "ATM",
      "ATS",
      "ADX"
    ],
    "status": "publicada",
    "source": "import",
    "imagenes": []
  },
  {
    "id": "q_la_ATP_2026_9766",
    "fuente": "ATP",
    "capitulo": 3,
    "capituloTitulo": "Aerodynamics",
    "seccion": "High-Lift Devices",
    "materia": "operaciones",
    "text": "On which type of wing are flaps most effective?",
    "options": [
      "Thin wing.",
      "Thick wing.",
      "Sweptback wing."
    ],
    "correctIndex": 1,
    "explanation": "The effectiveness of flaps on a wing configuration depends on many different factors, of which an important one is the amount of the wing area affected by the flaps. Since a certain amount of the span is reserved for ailerons, the actual wing maximum lift properties will be less than that resulting from the flapped two-dimensional section. If the basic wing has a low thickness, any type of flap will be less effective than on a wing of greater thickness. Sweepback of the wing can also cause a significant reduction in the effectiveness of flaps. (PLT266, AA.I.A.K12) — ANA",
    "cite": "ASA Airline Transport Pilot Test Prep 2025–2026 · Chapter 3 Aerodynamics · p. 3-26 · question 9766 · ATM, ATS, ADX",
