  {
    "id": "q_la_ATP_2026_8418",
    "fuente": "ATP",
    "capitulo": 3,
    "capituloTitulo": "Aerodynamics",
    "seccion": "Helicopter Aerodynamics",
    "materia": "operaciones",
    "text": "How is helicopter climb performance most adversely affected?",
    "options": [
      "Higher-than-standard temperature and high relative humidity.",
      "Lower-than-standard temperature and high relative humidity.",
      "Higher-than-standard temperature and low relative humidity."
    ],
    "correctIndex": 0,
    "explanation": "High elevations, high temperatures, and high moisture content (relative humidity) all contribute to high density altitude, which lessens helicopter performance. The thinner air at high density altitudes reduces the amount of lift of the rotor blades, and unsupercharged engines produce less power. (PLT124) — FAA-H-8083-21",
    "cite": "ASA Airline Transport Pilot Test Prep 2025–2026 · Chapter 3 Aerodynamics · p. 3-29 · question 8418 · RTC",
    "sourceQuestionId": "8418",
    "sourceEdition": "2025–2026",
    "sourcePage": 29,
    "sourceCategories": [
      "RTC"
    ],
    "status": "publicada",
    "source": "import",
    "imagenes": []
  },
  {
    "id": "q_la_ATP_2026_8420",
    "fuente": "ATP",
    "capitulo": 3,
    "capituloTitulo": "Aerodynamics",
    "seccion": "Helicopter Aerodynamics",
    "materia": "operaciones",
    "text": "What causes Coriolis effect?",
    "options": [
      "Differential thrust of rotor blades.",
      "Changing angle of attack of blades during rotation.",
      "Shift in center of mass of flapping blade."
    ],
    "correctIndex": 2,
    "explanation": "When a rotor blade of a three-bladed rotor system flaps upward, the center mass of that blade moves closer to the axis of rotation and blade acceleration takes place. When the blade flaps downward, its center of mass moves further from the axis of rotation and blade deceleration (or slowing) occurs. This increase and decrease of blade velocity in the plane of rotation due to mass movement is known as Coriolis effect. (PLT197) — FAA-H-8083-21",
    "cite": "ASA Airline Transport Pilot Test Prep 2025–2026 · Chapter 3 Aerodynamics · p. 3-29 · question 8420 · RTC",
    "sourceQuestionId": "8420",
    "sourceEdition": "2025–2026",
    "sourcePage": 29,
    "sourceCategories": [
      "RTC"
    ],
    "status": "publicada",
    "source": "import",
    "imagenes": []
  },
  {
    "id": "q_la_ATP_2026_8421",
    "fuente": "ATP",
    "capitulo": 3,
    "capituloTitulo": "Aerodynamics",
    "seccion": "Helicopter Aerodynamics",
    "materia": "operaciones",
    "text": "Why are the rotor blades more efficient when operating in ground effect?",
    "options": [
      "Induced drag is reduced.",
      "Induced angle of attack is increased.",
      "Downwash velocity is accelerated."
    ],
    "correctIndex": 0,
    "explanation": "When a helicopter is operated near the surface, the downwash velocity of the rotor blades cannot be fully developed. The reduction in downwash velocity causes the induced angle of attack of each rotor blade to be reduced, which causes the induced drag to be less. (PLT237) — FAA-H-8083-21",
    "cite": "ASA Airline Transport Pilot Test Prep 2025–2026 · Chapter 3 Aerodynamics · p. 3-29 · question 8421 · RTC",
    "sourceQuestionId": "8421",
    "sourceEdition": "2025–2026",
    "sourcePage": 29,
    "sourceCategories": [
      "RTC"
    ],
    "status": "publicada",
    "source": "import",
    "imagenes": []
  },
  {
    "id": "q_la_ATP_2026_8422",
    "fuente": "ATP",
    "capitulo": 3,
    "capituloTitulo": "Aerodynamics",
    "seccion": "Helicopter Aerodynamics",
    "materia": "operaciones",
    "text": "What result does a level turn have on the total lift required and load factor with a constant airspeed?",
    "options": [
      "Lift required remains constant and the load factor increases.",
      "Lift required increases and the load factor decreases.",
      "Both total lift force and load factor increase."
    ],
    "correctIndex": 2,
    "explanation": "When a helicopter is placed in a bank, the resultant lifting force acts more horizontally and less vertically. To maintain a level turn, the resultant lifting force (total lift force) must be increased. When a helicopter assumes a curved flight path, centrifugal force causes additional stresses (load factor) to be imposed. (PLT248) — FAA-H-8083-21",
    "cite": "ASA Airline Transport Pilot Test Prep 2025–2026 · Chapter 3 Aerodynamics · p. 3-30 · question 8422 · RTC",
    "sourceQuestionId": "8422",
    "sourceEdition": "2025–2026",
    "sourcePage": 30,
    "sourceCategories": [
      "RTC"
    ],
    "status": "publicada",
    "source": "import",
    "imagenes": []
  },
  {
    "id": "q_la_ATP_2026_8423",
    "fuente": "ATP",
    "capitulo": 3,
    "capituloTitulo": "Aerodynamics",
    "seccion": "Helicopter Aerodynamics",
    "materia": "operaciones",
    "text": "What causes a helicopter to turn?",
    "options": [
      "Centrifugal force.",
      "Horizontal component of lift.",
      "Greater angle of attack of rotor blades on upward side of the rotor disc."
    ],
    "correctIndex": 1,
    "explanation": "When a helicopter is placed in a bank, the rotor disc is tilted sideward causing the horizontal component of lift to be increased. The increased horizontal lift component pulls the helicopter from its straight course. (PLT248) — FAA-H-8083-21",
    "cite": "ASA Airline Transport Pilot Test Prep 2025–2026 · Chapter 3 Aerodynamics · p. 3-30 · question 8423 · RTC",
    "sourceQuestionId": "8423",
    "sourceEdition": "2025–2026",
    "sourcePage": 30,
    "sourceCategories": [
      "RTC"
    ],
    "status": "publicada",
    "source": "import",
    "imagenes": []
  },
  {
    "id": "q_la_ATP_2026_8424",
    "fuente": "ATP",
    "capitulo": 3,
    "capituloTitulo": "Aerodynamics",
    "seccion": "Helicopter Aerodynamics",
    "materia": "operaciones",
    "text": "What is the primary purpose of the tail rotor system?",
    "options": [
      "Maintain heading during forward flight.",
      "Act as a rudder to assist in coordinated turns.",
      "Counteract the torque effect of the main rotor."
    ],
    "correctIndex": 2,
    "explanation": "As the main rotor of a helicopter turns in one direction, the fuselage tends to rotate in the opposite direction (Newton’s third law of motion: for every action there is an equal and opposite reaction). This tendency to rotate is called torque. The tail rotor is used to produce thrust to counteract the torque effect of the main rotor. (PLT470) — FAA-H-8083-21",
    "cite": "ASA Airline Transport Pilot Test Prep 2025–2026 · Chapter 3 Aerodynamics · p. 3-30 · question 8424 · RTC",
    "sourceQuestionId": "8424",
    "sourceEdition": "2025–2026",
    "sourcePage": 30,
    "sourceCategories": [
      "RTC"
    ],
    "status": "publicada",
    "source": "import",
    "imagenes": []
  },
  {
    "id": "q_la_ATP_2026_8425",
    "fuente": "ATP",
    "capitulo": 3,
    "capituloTitulo": "Aerodynamics",
    "seccion": "Helicopter Aerodynamics",
    "materia": "operaciones",
    "text": "Under what condition would it be necessary to cause the tail rotor to direct thrust to the left on an American-made helicopter?",
    "options": [
      "To maintain heading with a left crosswind.",
      "To counteract the drag of the transmission during autorotation.",
      "To execute hovering turns to the right."
    ],
    "correctIndex": 1,
    "explanation": "The capability for tail rotors to produce thrust to the left (negative pitch angle) is necessary because during autorotation, the drag of the transmission (with no torque effect present) tends to yaw the nose to the left, in the same direction that the main rotor is turning. (PLT470) — FAA-H-8083-21 ”?",
    "cite": "ASA Airline Transport Pilot Test Prep 2025–2026 · Chapter 3 Aerodynamics · p. 3-30 · question 8425 · RTC",
    "sourceQuestionId": "8425",
    "sourceEdition": "2025–2026",
    "sourcePage": 30,
    "sourceCategories": [
      "RTC"
    ],
    "status": "publicada",
    "source": "import",
    "imagenes": []
  },
  {
