  },
  {
    "id": "q_la_ATP_2026_9018",
    "fuente": "ATP",
    "capitulo": 6,
    "capituloTitulo": "Flight Operations",
    "seccion": "Airspace",
    "materia": "operaciones",
    "text": "A minimum instrument altitude for enroute operations off of published airways which provides obstruction clearance of 1,000 feet in nonmountainous terrain areas and 2,000 feet in designated mountainous areas within the United States is called",
    "options": [
      "Minimum obstruction clearance altitude (MOCA).",
      "Off-route obstruction clearance altitude (OROCA).",
      "Minimum safe/sector altitude (MSA)."
    ],
    "correctIndex": 1,
    "explanation": "OROCA is an off-route altitude which provides obstruction clearance with a 1,000-foot buffer in non moun tainous terrain areas, and a 2,000-foot buffer in designated mountainous areas within the United States. (PLT162, AA.II.A.S6) — AIM ¶4-4-9 Answer (A) is incorrect because MOCAs provide the lowest published altitude in effect between radio fixes on VOR airways, off-airway routes, or route segments which meets obstacle clearance requirements for the entire route segment and which ensures acceptable navigational signal coverage only within 25 SM (22 NM) of a VOR. Answer (C) is incorrect because MSAs are published for emergency use on IAP charts; they are expressed in feet above mean sea level and normally have a 25 NM radius; however, this radius may be expanded to 30 NM if necessary to encompass the airport landing surfaces.",
    "cite": "ASA Airline Transport Pilot Test Prep 2025–2026 · Chapter 6 Flight Operations · p. 6-11 · question 9018 · ALL",
    "sourceQuestionId": "9018",
    "sourceEdition": "2025–2026",
    "sourcePage": 11,
    "sourceCategories": [
      "ALL"
    ],
    "status": "publicada",
    "source": "import",
    "imagenes": []
  },
  {
    "id": "q_la_ATP_2026_8893",
    "fuente": "ATP",
    "capitulo": 6,
    "capituloTitulo": "Flight Operations",
    "seccion": "Airspace",
    "materia": "operaciones",
    "text": "What is the required flight visibility and distance from clouds if you are operating in Class E airspace at 9,500 feet with a VFR-on-top clearance during daylight hours?",
    "options": [
      "3 statute miles, 1,000 feet above, 500 feet below, and 2,000 feet horizontal.",
      "5 statute miles, 500 feet above, 1,000 feet below, and 2,000 feet horizontal.",
      "3 statute miles, 500 feet above, 1,000 feet below, and 2,000 feet horizontal."
    ],
    "correctIndex": 0,
    "explanation": "A pilot receiving authorization for VFR-on-top must comply with VFR visibility, distance from cloud criteria, and minimum IFR altitudes. When operating at more than 1,200 feet AGL but less than 10,000 feet MSL, pilots are required to maintain flight visibility of 3 statute miles and a distance from clouds of 1,000 feet above, 500 feet below, and 2,000 feet horizontal. (PLT163, AA.I.G.K2) — 14 CFR §91.155 Answer (B) is incorrect because the visibility requirement is 3 miles (not 5 miles) and the distances from clouds above and below are reversed. They should be 1,000 feet above and 500 feet below. Answer (C) is incorrect because the distances from clouds above and below are reversed. They should be 1,000 feet above and 500 feet below.",
    "cite": "ASA Airline Transport Pilot Test Prep 2025–2026 · Chapter 6 Flight Operations · p. 6-11 · question 8893 · ALL",
    "sourceQuestionId": "8893",
    "sourceEdition": "2025–2026",
    "sourcePage": 11,
    "sourceCategories": [
      "ALL"
    ],
    "status": "publicada",
    "source": "import",
    "imagenes": []
  },
  {
    "id": "q_la_ATP_2026_8900",
    "fuente": "ATP",
    "capitulo": 6,
    "capituloTitulo": "Flight Operations",
    "seccion": "Airspace",
    "materia": "operaciones",
    "text": "What is the minimum flight visibility and distance from clouds for flight at 10,500 feet, in Class E airspace, with a VFR-on-top clearance during daylight hours?",
    "options": [
      "3 statute miles, 1,000 feet above, 500 feet below, and 2,000 feet horizontal.",
      "5 statute miles, 1,000 feet above, 1,000 feet below, and 1 mile horizontal.",
      "5 statute miles, 1,000 feet above, 500 feet below, and 1 mile horizontal."
    ],
    "correctIndex": 1,
    "explanation": "A pilot on an IFR flight plan requesting and receiving authorization to operate VFR-on-top must comply with instrument flight rules as well as VFR visibilities and distances from clouds. When operating at more than 1,200 feet AGL and at or above 10,000 feet MSL pilots are required to maintain flight visibility of 5 SM and distances of 1,000 feet above, 1,000 feet below, and 1 mile horizontally from clouds. (PLT163, AA.I.G.K2) — 14 CFR §91.155 Answer (A) is incorrect because it presents the VFR weather minimums for below 10,000 feet MSL. Answer (C) is incorrect because the vertical separation from clouds is 1,000 feet both above and below.",
    "cite": "ASA Airline Transport Pilot Test Prep 2025–2026 · Chapter 6 Flight Operations · p. 6-11 · question 8900 · ALL",
    "sourceQuestionId": "8900",
    "sourceEdition": "2025–2026",
    "sourcePage": 11,
    "sourceCategories": [
