  {
    "id": "q_la_ATP_2026_9396",
    "fuente": "ATP",
    "capitulo": 6,
    "capituloTitulo": "Flight Operations",
    "seccion": "Airspace",
    "materia": "operaciones",
    "text": "What is the maximum indicated airspeed a reciprocating-engine-powered airplane may be operated within Class B airspace?",
    "options": [
      "180 knots.",
      "230 knots.",
      "250 knots."
    ],
    "correctIndex": 2,
    "explanation": "Unless otherwise authorized by the Administrator, no person may operate an aircraft below 10,000 feet MSL at an indicated airspeed of more than 250 knots (288 mph). There is no specific speed restriction which applies to operation within Class B airspace. (PLT161, AA.I.G.K2) — 14 CFR §91.117 Answer (A) is incorrect because 180 knots is the old maximum airspeed for turbine-powered aircraft while operating within Class D airspace (it is now 200 knots). Answer (B) is incorrect because 230 knots is the maximum authorized holding speed for all civil turbojet aircraft while operating from the minimum holding altitude to 14,000 feet. It is not an airspeed limitation in Class B airspace.",
