  {
    "id": "q_la_ATP_2026_9091",
    "fuente": "ATP",
    "capitulo": 6,
    "capituloTitulo": "Flight Operations",
    "seccion": "Instrument Approaches",
    "materia": "operaciones",
    "text": "What is the difference between a visual and a contact approach?",
    "options": [
      "A visual approach is an IFR authorization while a contact approach is a VFR authorization.",
      "A visual approach is initiated by ATC while a contact approach is initiated by the pilot.",
      "Both are the same but classified according to the party initiating the approach."
    ],
    "correctIndex": 1,
    "explanation": "Visual approaches are initiated by ATC to reduce pilot/ controller workload and expedite traffic. Pilots operating on IFR flight plans may request a contact approach if the appropriate weather conditions exist. (PLT170, AA.VI.E.K4) — AIM ¶5-4-23, 5-4-25 Answer (A) is incorrect because both a visual and contact approach are initiated from an IFR flight plan. Answer (C) is incorrect because a visual approach is one in which the pilot has a preceding aircraft or the airport in sight and can maintain basic VFR weather minimums. A contact approach is used by a pilot in lieu of conducting a standard or special instrument approach to an airport.",
    "cite": "ASA Airline Transport Pilot Test Prep 2025–2026 · Chapter 6 Flight Operations · p. 6-30 · question 9091 · ALL",
    "sourceQuestionId": "9091",
    "sourceEdition": "2025–2026",
    "sourcePage": 30,
    "sourceCategories": [
      "ALL"
    ],
    "status": "publicada",
    "source": "import",
    "imagenes": []
  },
  {
    "id": "q_la_ATP_2026_8953",
    "fuente": "ATP",
    "capitulo": 6,
    "capituloTitulo": "Flight Operations",
    "seccion": "Instrument Approaches",
    "materia": "operaciones",
    "text": "When simultaneous approaches are in progress, how does each pilot receive radar advisories?",
    "options": [
      "On tower frequency.",
      "On approach control frequency.",
      "One pilot on tower frequency and the other on approach control frequency."
    ],
    "correctIndex": 0,
    "explanation": "Whenever simultaneous approaches are in progress, radar advisories will be provided on the tower frequency. (PLT420, AA.VI.C.K3) — AIM ¶5-4-15 Answer (B) is incorrect because pilots will be advised to monitor the tower (not approach control) frequency to receive radar advisories and instructions. Answer (C) is incorrect because both pilots would receive radar advisories on the tower frequency.",
    "cite": "ASA Airline Transport Pilot Test Prep 2025–2026 · Chapter 6 Flight Operations · p. 6-30 · question 8953 · ALL",
    "sourceQuestionId": "8953",
    "sourceEdition": "2025–2026",
    "sourcePage": 30,
    "sourceCategories": [
      "ALL"
    ],
    "status": "publicada",
    "source": "import",
    "imagenes": []
  },
  {
    "id": "q_la_ATP_2026_8955",
    "fuente": "ATP",
    "capitulo": 6,
    "capituloTitulo": "Flight Operations",
    "seccion": "Instrument Approaches",
    "materia": "operaciones",
    "text": "When simultaneous ILS approaches are in progress, which of the following should approach control be advised of immediately?",
    "options": [
      "Any inoperative or malfunctioning aircraft receivers.",
      "If a simultaneous ILS approach is desired.",
      "If radar monitoring is desired to confirm lateral separation."
    ],
    "correctIndex": 0,
    "explanation": "When advised that simultaneous ILS approaches are in progress, pilots shall advise approach control immediately of malfunctioning or inoperative receivers or if simultaneous approach is not desired. (PLT170, AA.VI.E.K1) — AIM ¶5-4-15 Answer (B) is incorrect because simultaneous approaches are issued at any time according to ATC needs, and it is not the responsibility of the pilot to request such an approach. Answer (C) is incorrect because radar monitoring is always provided during simultaneous approaches.",
    "cite": "ASA Airline Transport Pilot Test Prep 2025–2026 · Chapter 6 Flight Operations · p. 6-30 · question 8955 · ALL",
    "sourceQuestionId": "8955",
    "sourceEdition": "2025–2026",
    "sourcePage": 30,
    "sourceCategories": [
      "ALL"
    ],
    "status": "publicada",
    "source": "import",
    "imagenes": []
  },
  {
    "id": "q_la_ATP_2026_8954",
    "fuente": "ATP",
    "capitulo": 6,
    "capituloTitulo": "Flight Operations",
    "seccion": "Instrument Approaches",
    "materia": "operaciones",
    "text": "When cleared to execute a published side-step maneuver, at what point is the pilot expected to commence this maneuver?",
    "options": [
      "At the published DH.",
      "At the MDA published or a circling approach.",
      "As soon as possible after the runway environment is in sight."
    ],
    "correctIndex": 2,
    "explanation": "Pilots are expected to execute the side-step maneuver as soon as possible after the runway or runway environment is in sight. (PLT083, AA.VI.D.K1) — AIM ¶5-4-19 Answers (A) and (B) are incorrect because the side-step maneuver can only be performed and should be performed as soon as possible after the runway or runway environment is in sight.",
    "cite": "ASA Airline Transport Pilot Test Prep 2025–2026 · Chapter 6 Flight Operations · p. 6-30 · question 8954 · ALL",
    "sourceQuestionId": "8954",
    "sourceEdition": "2025–2026",
    "sourcePage": 30,
    "sourceCategories": [
      "ALL"
    ],
    "status": "publicada",
    "source": "import",
    "imagenes": []
  },
  {
    "id": "q_la_ATP_2026_9438",
    "fuente": "ATP",
    "capitulo": 6,
    "capituloTitulo": "Flight Operations",
    "seccion": "Instrument Approaches",
    "materia": "operaciones",
    "text": "When cleared to execute a published side-step maneuver for a specific approach and landing on the parallel runway, at what point is the pilot expected to commence this maneuver?",
    "options": [
      "At the published minimum altitude for a circling approach.",
      "As soon as possible after the runway or runway environment is in sight.",
      "At the localizer MDA minimums and when the runway is in sight."
    ],
    "correctIndex": 1,
    "explanation": "Pilots are expected to execute the side-step maneuver as soon as possible after the runway or runway environment is in sight. (PLT170, AA.VI.E.K4) — AIM ¶5-4-19 Answers (A) and (C) are incorrect because the maneuver should be started as soon as the runway environment is in sight not at a DH or MDA of an approach.",
    "cite": "ASA Airline Transport Pilot Test Prep 2025–2026 · Chapter 6 Flight Operations · p. 6-30 · question 9438 · ATM, ATS, RTC",
    "sourceQuestionId": "9438",
    "sourceEdition": "2025–2026",
