  {
    "id": "q_la_ATP_2026_9012",
    "fuente": "ATP",
    "capitulo": 6,
    "capituloTitulo": "Flight Operations",
    "seccion": "Charts",
    "materia": "operaciones",
    "text": "In what way are SIDs depicted in plan view?",
    "options": [
      "“Vectors” provided for navigational guidance or “Pilot NAV” with courses the pilot is responsible to follow.",
      "“Vectors” and “Pilot NAV” for pilots to use at their discretion.",
      "Combined textual and graphic form which are mandatory routes and instructions."
    ],
    "correctIndex": 0,
    "explanation": "Pilot NAV DPs (previously called SIDs) are established when the pilot is primarily responsible for navigation on the DP route. Vector DPs are established when ATC will provide radar navigational guidance to a filed/ assigned route or to a fix depicted on the DP. (PLT201, AA.VI.C.K1) — AIM ¶5-2-9 Answer (B) is incorrect because DPs must be followed as depicted (not at the pilot’s discretion). If a pilot does not wish to use a DP, then they must notify ATC. Answer (C) is incorrect because a NOS DP does not list the textual description in the plan view. The plan view depicts a DP as either a pilot NAV or vector to signify if navigation is provided by the pilot or by radar vectors.",
    "cite": "ASA Airline Transport Pilot Test Prep 2025–2026 · Chapter 6 Flight Operations · p. 6-49 · question 9012 · ALL",
    "sourceQuestionId": "9012",
    "sourceEdition": "2025–2026",
    "sourcePage": 49,
    "sourceCategories": [
      "ALL"
    ],
    "status": "publicada",
    "source": "import",
    "imagenes": []
  },
  {
    "id": "q_la_ATP_2026_9034",
    "fuente": "ATP",
    "capitulo": 6,
    "capituloTitulo": "Flight Operations",
    "seccion": "Charts",
    "materia": "operaciones",
    "text": "What is the primary purpose of a STAR?",
    "options": [
      "Provide separation between IFR and VFR traffic.",
      "Simplify clearance delivery procedures.",
      "Decrease traffic congestion at certain airports."
    ],
    "correctIndex": 1,
    "explanation": "A STAR is an ATC-coded IFR arrival route established for arriving IFR aircraft destined for certain airports. Its purpose is to simplify clearance delivery procedures. (PLT170, AA.VI.C.K1) — AIM ¶5-4-1 Answer (A) is incorrect because separation between IFR and VFR traffic is provided by radar services, not a STAR. Answer (C) is incorrect because controlled airspace, e.g., Class B, Class C, can be used to decrease traffic congestion at some airports by allowing ATC to regulate traffic flow and volume.",
    "cite": "ASA Airline Transport Pilot Test Prep 2025–2026 · Chapter 6 Flight Operations · p. 6-49 · question 9034 · ALL",
    "sourceQuestionId": "9034",
    "sourceEdition": "2025–2026",
    "sourcePage": 49,
    "sourceCategories": [
