  {
    "id": "q_la_ATP_2026_9097",
    "fuente": "ATP",
    "capitulo": 7,
    "capituloTitulo": "Emergencies, Hazards, and Flight Physiology",
    "seccion": "Flight Emergencies and Hazards",
    "materia": "operaciones",
    "text": "What minimum condition is suggested for declaring an emergency?",
    "options": [
      "Anytime the pilot is doubtful of a condition that could adversely affect flight safety.",
      "When fuel endurance or weather will require an en route or landing priority.",
      "When distress conditions such as fire, mechanical failure, or structural damage occurs."
    ],
    "correctIndex": 0,
    "explanation": "An aircraft is in an emergency condition the moment the pilot becomes doubtful about position, fuel endurance, weather, or any other condition that could adversely affect flight safety. (PLT394, AA.VII.A.K2) — AIM ¶6-1-2 Answer (B) is incorrect because, if fuel endurance or weather will require an en route or landing priority, this is beyond an urgency situation and is now a distress condition. Answer (C) is incorrect because this is the step after an urgency condition, one that has the potential to become a distress condition.",
    "cite": "ASA Airline Transport Pilot Test Prep 2025–2026 · Chapter 7 Emergencies, Hazards, and Flight Physiology · p. 7-5 · question 9097 · ALL",
    "sourceQuestionId": "9097",
    "sourceEdition": "2025–2026",
    "sourcePage": 5,
    "sourceCategories": [
      "ALL"
    ],
    "status": "publicada",
    "source": "import",
    "imagenes": []
  },
  {
    "id": "q_la_ATP_2026_9051",
    "fuente": "ATP",
    "capitulo": 7,
    "capituloTitulo": "Emergencies, Hazards, and Flight Physiology",
    "seccion": "Flight Emergencies and Hazards",
    "materia": "operaciones",
    "text": "What is the hijack code?",
    "options": [
      "7200.",
      "7500.",
      "7777."
    ],
    "correctIndex": 1,
    "explanation": "Only nondiscrete transponder code 7500 will be decoded as the hijack code. (PLT497, AA.I.A.K9) — AIM ¶4-1-20 Answer (A) is incorrect because 7200 is a code that is used for normal operating procedures. Answer (C) is incorrect because code 7777 is reserved for military interceptor operations.",
    "cite": "ASA Airline Transport Pilot Test Prep 2025–2026 · Chapter 7 Emergencies, Hazards, and Flight Physiology · p. 7-5 · question 9051 · ALL",
    "sourceQuestionId": "9051",
    "sourceEdition": "2025–2026",
