  {
    "id": "q_la_ATP_2026_9010",
    "fuente": "ATP",
    "capitulo": 7,
    "capituloTitulo": "Emergencies, Hazards, and Flight Physiology",
    "seccion": "Flight Emergencies and Hazards",
    "materia": "operaciones",
    "text": "Under what condition should a pilot on IFR advise ATC of minimum fuel status?",
    "options": [
      "When the fuel supply becomes less than that required for IFR.",
      "If the remaining fuel suggests a need for traffic or landing priority.",
      "If the remaining fuel precludes any undue delay."
    ],
    "correctIndex": 2,
    "explanation": "A pilot should advise ATC of their minimum fuel status when the fuel supply has reached a state where, upon reaching destination, the pilot cannot accept any undue delay. It indicates a possible future emergency, but does not declare one and does not get priority handling. (PLT318, AA.VI.J.K2) — AIM ¶5-5-15 Answer (A) is incorrect because a pilot must ensure the minimum amount of fuel is on board the airplane for the planned IFR flight and alternatives, if needed, during the flight planning phase. Answer (B) is incorrect because, if the remaining fuel suggests a need for traffic or landing priority, the pilot should declare an emergency (not minimum fuel status), and report fuel remaining in minutes.",
    "cite": "ASA Airline Transport Pilot Test Prep 2025–2026 · Chapter 7 Emergencies, Hazards, and Flight Physiology · p. 7-7 · question 9010 · ALL",
    "sourceQuestionId": "9010",
    "sourceEdition": "2025–2026",
    "sourcePage": 7,
    "sourceCategories": [
      "ALL"
    ],
    "status": "publicada",
    "source": "import",
    "imagenes": []
  },
  {
    "id": "q_la_ATP_2026_9011",
    "fuente": "ATP",
    "capitulo": 7,
    "capituloTitulo": "Emergencies, Hazards, and Flight Physiology",
    "seccion": "Flight Emergencies and Hazards",
    "materia": "operaciones",
    "text": "What does the term “minimum fuel” imply to ATC?",
    "options": [
      "Traffic priority is needed to the destination airport.",
      "Emergency handling is required to the nearest suitable airport.",
      "Advisory that indicates an emergency situation is possible should an undue delay occur."
    ],
    "correctIndex": 2,
    "explanation": "A pilot should advise ATC of their minimum fuel status when the fuel supply has reached a state where, upon reaching destination, the pilot cannot accept any undue delay. It indicates a possible future emergency, but does not declare one and does not get priority handling. (PLT318, AA.VI.J.K2) — AIM ¶5-5-15 Answer (A) is incorrect because a pilot should declare an emergency and report fuel remaining in minutes if traffic priority is needed to the destination airport. Answer (B) is incorrect because emergency handling to the nearest suitable airport would be required in a distress or urgent type of emergency.",
    "cite": "ASA Airline Transport Pilot Test Prep 2025–2026 · Chapter 7 Emergencies, Hazards, and Flight Physiology · p. 7-7 · question 9011 · ALL",
    "sourceQuestionId": "9011",
    "sourceEdition": "2025–2026",
