  {
    "id": "q_la_ATP_2026_9050",
    "fuente": "ATP",
    "capitulo": 7,
    "capituloTitulo": "Emergencies, Hazards, and Flight Physiology",
    "seccion": "Flight Emergencies and Hazards",
    "materia": "operaciones",
    "text": "What conditions would cause an air traffic controller to issue you a safety alert?",
    "options": [
      "When your approach has become unstable, and you are required to execute a go-around.",
      "When the aircraft altitude places it in unsafe proximity to terrain, obstructions, or other aircraft.",
      "When they have implemented a temporary reduction in approach control separation minimums."
    ],
    "correctIndex": 1,
    "explanation": "A safety alert will be issued to pilots of aircraft being controlled by ATC if the controller is aware the aircraft is at an altitude which, in the controller’s judgment, places the aircraft in unsafe proximity to terrain, obstructions or other aircraft. (PLT172, AA.VI.J.K2) — AIM ¶4-1-16 Answer (A) is incorrect because a safety alert is issued to a pilot if ATC believes that his/her airplane is at an altitude which would place it in unsafe proximity to another airplane. Answer (C) is incorrect because, when weather conditions are extreme and wind shear or large hail is in the vicinity, a Convective SIGMET would be broadcast.",
    "cite": "ASA Airline Transport Pilot Test Prep 2025–2026 · Chapter 7 Emergencies, Hazards, and Flight Physiology · p. 7-8 · question 9050 · ALL",
