  },
  {
    "id": "q_la_ATP_2026_9120",
    "fuente": "ATP",
    "capitulo": 7,
    "capituloTitulo": "Emergencies, Hazards, and Flight Physiology",
    "seccion": "Flight Emergencies and Hazards",
    "materia": "operaciones",
    "text": "Hazardous vortex turbulence that might be encountered behind large aircraft is created only when that aircraft is",
    "options": [
      "developing lift.",
      "operating at high airspeeds.",
      "using high power settings."
    ],
    "correctIndex": 0,
    "explanation": "Lift is generated by the creation of a pressure differential over the wing surface. The lowest pressure occurs over the upper wing surface and the highest pressure under the wing. This pressure differential triggers the roll up of the airflow aft of the wing resulting in swirling air masses trailing downstream of the wing tips. (PLT509, AA.VI.A.K1) — AIM ¶7-4-2 Answer (B) is incorrect because hazardous vortex turbulence is created only when the aircraft is developing lift, which can be while operating at low or high airspeeds. A slow, heavy, and clean airplane will generate the most hazardous vortex turbulence. Answer (C) is incorrect because most takeoff rolls are at high power settings, but the generation of vortices does not occur until lift is produced. Landing approaches are also conducted at lower power settings; vortex turbulence is produced whenever an airplane is producing lift.",
    "cite": "ASA Airline Transport Pilot Test Prep 2025–2026 · Chapter 7 Emergencies, Hazards, and Flight Physiology · p. 7-9 · question 9120 · ALL",
    "sourceQuestionId": "9120",
    "sourceEdition": "2025–2026",
    "sourcePage": 9,
    "sourceCategories": [
      "ALL"
    ],
    "status": "publicada",
    "source": "import",
    "imagenes": []
  },
  {
    "id": "q_la_ATP_2026_9121",
    "fuente": "ATP",
    "capitulo": 7,
    "capituloTitulo": "Emergencies, Hazards, and Flight Physiology",
    "seccion": "Flight Emergencies and Hazards",
    "materia": "operaciones",
    "text": "Wingtip vortices created by large aircraft tend to",
    "options": [
      "sink below the aircraft generating the turbulence.",
      "rise from the surface to traffic pattern altitude.",
      "accumulate and remain for a period of time at the point where the takeoff roll began."
    ],
    "correctIndex": 0,
    "explanation": "Flight tests have shown that the vortices from large aircraft sink at a rate of several hundred feet per minute. They tend to level off at a distance about 900 feet below the flight path of the generating aircraft. (PLT509, AA.VI.A.K1) — AIM ¶7-4-4 Answer (B) is incorrect because vortices created by large aircraft tend to sink from (not rise into) the traffic pattern altitude. Answer (C) is incorrect because wing-tip vortices are not generated until the aircraft’s wings develop lift, so no wing-tip vortices are generated at the point where the takeoff roll begins.",
    "cite": "ASA Airline Transport Pilot Test Prep 2025–2026 · Chapter 7 Emergencies, Hazards, and Flight Physiology · p. 7-9 · question 9121 · ALL",
    "sourceQuestionId": "9121",
    "sourceEdition": "2025–2026",
    "sourcePage": 9,
    "sourceCategories": [
      "ALL"
    ],
    "status": "publicada",
    "source": "import",
    "imagenes": []
  },
  {
    "id": "q_la_ATP_2026_9122",
    "fuente": "ATP",
    "capitulo": 7,
    "capituloTitulo": "Emergencies, Hazards, and Flight Physiology",
    "seccion": "Flight Emergencies and Hazards",
    "materia": "operaciones",
    "text": "How does the wake turbulence vortex circulate around each wingtip?",
    "options": [
      "Inward, upward, and around the wingtip.",
      "Counterclockwise when viewed from behind the aircraft.",
      "Outward, upward, and around the wingtip."
    ],
    "correctIndex": 2,
    "explanation": "The vortex circulation is outward, upward, and around the wing tips when viewed from either ahead or behind the aircraft. (PLT509, AA.VI.A.K1) — AIM ¶7-4-4",
    "cite": "ASA Airline Transport Pilot Test Prep 2025–2026 · Chapter 7 Emergencies, Hazards, and Flight Physiology · p. 7-9 · question 9122 · ALL",
    "sourceQuestionId": "9122",
    "sourceEdition": "2025–2026",
    "sourcePage": 9,
    "sourceCategories": [
      "ALL"
    ],
    "status": "publicada",
    "source": "import",
    "imagenes": []
  },
  {
    "id": "q_la_ATP_2026_9123",
    "fuente": "ATP",
    "capitulo": 7,
    "capituloTitulo": "Emergencies, Hazards, and Flight Physiology",
    "seccion": "Flight Emergencies and Hazards",
    "materia": "operaciones",
    "text": "Which statement is true concerning the wake turbulence produced by a large transport aircraft?",
    "options": [
      "Vortices can be avoided by flying 300 feet below and behind the flightpath of the generating aircraft.",
      "The vortex characteristics of any given aircraft may be altered by extending the flaps or changing the speed.",
      "Wake turbulence behind a propeller-driven aircraft is negligible because jet engine thrust is a necessary factor in the formation of vortices."
    ],
    "correctIndex": 1,
    "explanation": "The strength of the vortex is governed by the weight, speed and shape of the wing of the generating aircraft. The vortex characteristics of a given aircraft can be changed by extension of flaps or other wing configuring devices as well as by a change in speed. (PLT509, AA.VI.A.K1) — AIM ¶7-4-3 Answer (A) is incorrect because the vortices generated by large transport aircraft tend to sink below and behind, thus vortices can be expected by flying 300 feet below and behind the flight path of the generating aircraft. Answer (C) is incorrect because wake turbulence vortices are generated by lift produced by any airplane. It does not matter whether the airplane is powered by propeller or jet engines.",
    "cite": "ASA Airline Transport Pilot Test Prep 2025–2026 · Chapter 7 Emergencies, Hazards, and Flight Physiology · p. 7-9 · question 9123 · ALL",
    "sourceQuestionId": "9123",
    "sourceEdition": "2025–2026",
    "sourcePage": 9,
    "sourceCategories": [
      "ALL"
    ],
    "status": "publicada",
    "source": "import",
    "imagenes": []
  },
  {
    "id": "q_la_ATP_2026_9124",
    "fuente": "ATP",
    "capitulo": 7,
    "capituloTitulo": "Emergencies, Hazards, and Flight Physiology",
    "seccion": "Flight Emergencies and Hazards",
    "materia": "operaciones",
    "text": "What effect would a light crosswind have on the wingtip vortices generated by a large airplane that has just taken off?",
    "options": [
      "The upwind vortex will tend to remain on the runway longer than the downwind vortex.",
      "A crosswind will rapidly dissipate the strength of both vortices.",
      "The downwind vortex will tend to remain on the runway longer than the upwind vortex."
    ],
    "correctIndex": 0,
    "explanation": "A crosswind will decrease the lateral movement of the upwind vortex and increase the movement of the downwind vortex. Thus, a light wind of 3 to 7 knots could result in the upwind vortex remaining in the touchdown zone for a period of time and hasten the drift of the downwind vortex toward another runway. (PLT509, AA.VI.A.K1) — AIM ¶7-4-4 Answer (B) is incorrect because a crosswind will hold the upwind vortex in the vicinity of the runway. Answer (C) is incorrect because the downwind vortex moves away at a faster rate than the upwind vortex.",
    "cite": "ASA Airline Transport Pilot Test Prep 2025–2026 · Chapter 7 Emergencies, Hazards, and Flight Physiology · p. 7-9 · question 9124 · ALL",
    "sourceQuestionId": "9124",
    "sourceEdition": "2025–2026",
    "sourcePage": 9,
    "sourceCategories": [
      "ALL"
    ],
    "status": "publicada",
    "source": "import",
    "imagenes": []
  },
  {
    "id": "q_la_ATP_2026_9125",
    "fuente": "ATP",
    "capitulo": 7,
    "capituloTitulo": "Emergencies, Hazards, and Flight Physiology",
    "seccion": "Flight Emergencies and Hazards",
    "materia": "operaciones",
    "text": "To avoid the wingtip vortices of a departing jet airplane during takeoff, the pilot should",
    "options": [
      "lift off at a point well past the jet airplane’s flightpath.",
      "climb above and stay upwind of the jet airplane’s flightpath.",
      "remain below the flightpath of the jet airplane."
    ],
    "correctIndex": 1,
    "explanation": "When departing behind a large aircraft, note its rotation point and rotate prior to that point. During the climb, stay above and upwind of the large aircraft’s climb path until turning clear of its wake. (PLT509, AA.VI.A.R2) — AIM ¶7-4-4 Answer (A) is incorrect because, if you rotate beyond the jet’s rotation point, you will have to fly up into the jet’s vortices. Answer (C) is incorrect because the jet’s vortices will sink. If you stay below the jet’s flight path, you will fly in the area of the vortices.",
    "cite": "ASA Airline Transport Pilot Test Prep 2025–2026 · Chapter 7 Emergencies, Hazards, and Flight Physiology · p. 7-10 · question 9125 · ALL",
    "sourceQuestionId": "9125",
    "sourceEdition": "2025–2026",
    "sourcePage": 10,
    "sourceCategories": [
      "ALL"
    ],
    "status": "publicada",
    "source": "import",
    "imagenes": []
  },
  {
    "id": "q_la_ATP_2026_9126",
    "fuente": "ATP",
    "capitulo": 7,
    "capituloTitulo": "Emergencies, Hazards, and Flight Physiology",
    "seccion": "Flight Emergencies and Hazards",
    "materia": "operaciones",
    "text": "What wind condition prolongs the hazards of wake turbulence on a landing runway for the longest period of time?",
    "options": [
      "Direct tailwind.",
      "Light quartering tailwind.",
      "Light quartering headwind."
    ],
    "correctIndex": 1,
    "explanation": "A crosswind will decrease the lateral movement of the upwind vortex and increase the movement of the downwind vortex. Thus a light wind of 3 to 7 knots could result in the upwind vortex remaining in the touchdown zone for a period of time and hasten the drift of the downwind vortex toward another runway. Similarly, a tailwind condition can move the vortices of the preceding aircraft forward into the touchdown zone. The light, quartering tailwind requires maximum caution. (PLT509, AA.VI.A.R2) — AIM ¶7-4-4 Answer (A) is incorrect because, even though a direct tailwind can move the vortices of a preceding aircraft forward into the touchdown zone, it is not as hazardous because both vortices would move to the sides (and not remain on the runway). Answer (C) is incorrect because a light quartering headwind would move the vortices toward the runway threshold, away from (not into) the touchdown zone on a landing runway.",
    "cite": "ASA Airline Transport Pilot Test Prep 2025–2026 · Chapter 7 Emergencies, Hazards, and Flight Physiology · p. 7-10 · question 9126 · ALL",
    "sourceQuestionId": "9126",
    "sourceEdition": "2025–2026",
    "sourcePage": 10,
    "sourceCategories": [
      "ALL"
    ],
    "status": "publicada",
    "source": "import",
    "imagenes": []
  },
  {
    "id": "q_la_ATP_2026_9127",
    "fuente": "ATP",
    "capitulo": 7,
    "capituloTitulo": "Emergencies, Hazards, and Flight Physiology",
    "seccion": "Flight Emergencies and Hazards",
    "materia": "operaciones",
    "text": "If you take off behind a heavy jet that has just landed, you should plan to lift off",
    "options": [
      "prior to the point where the jet touched down.",
      "beyond the point where the jet touched down.",
      "at the point where the jet touched down and on the upwind edge of the runway."
    ],
    "correctIndex": 1,
    "explanation": "When departing behind a large aircraft which has just landed, note the aircraft’s touchdown point and rotate past that point on the runway. (PLT509, AA.VI.A.R2) — AIM ¶7-4-6 Answer (A) is incorrect because a lift-off prior to the point where the jet touched down would force you to climb through the jet’s vortices. Answer (C) is incorrect because lift-off should be planned beyond the point of touchdown to ensure that you avoid the vortices, and you should remain on the center of the runway during takeoff.",
    "cite": "ASA Airline Transport Pilot Test Prep 2025–2026 · Chapter 7 Emergencies, Hazards, and Flight Physiology · p. 7-10 · question 9127 · ALL",
    "sourceQuestionId": "9127",
    "sourceEdition": "2025–2026",
    "sourcePage": 10,
    "sourceCategories": [
      "ALL"
    ],
    "status": "publicada",
    "source": "import",
    "imagenes": []
  },
  {
    "id": "q_la_ATP_2026_9715",
    "fuente": "ATP",
    "capitulo": 7,
    "capituloTitulo": "Emergencies, Hazards, and Flight Physiology",
    "seccion": "Flight Emergencies and Hazards",
    "materia": "operaciones",
    "text": "To allow pilots of in-trail lighter aircraft to make flight path adjustments to avoid wake turbulence, pilots of heavy and large jet aircraft should fly",
    "options": [
      "below the established glidepath and slightly to either side of the on-course centerline.",
      "on the established glidepath and on the approach course centerline or runway centerline extended.",
      "above the established glidepath and slightly downwind of the on-course centerline."
    ],
    "correctIndex": 1,
    "explanation": "Pilots of aircraft that produce strong wake vortices should make every attempt to fly on the established glidepath, and as closely as possible to the approach course centerline or to the extended centerline of the runway of intended landing. (PLT509, AA.VI.A.R2) — AIM ¶7-4-8",
    "cite": "ASA Airline Transport Pilot Test Prep 2025–2026 · Chapter 7 Emergencies, Hazards, and Flight Physiology · p. 7-10 · question 9715 · ALL",
    "sourceQuestionId": "9715",
    "sourceEdition": "2025–2026",
    "sourcePage": 10,
    "sourceCategories": [
      "ALL"
    ],
    "status": "publicada",
    "source": "import",
    "imagenes": []
  },
  {
    "id": "q_la_ATP_2026_9858",
    "fuente": "ATP",
    "capitulo": 7,
    "capituloTitulo": "Emergencies, Hazards, and Flight Physiology",
    "seccion": "Flight Emergencies and Hazards",
    "materia": "operaciones",
    "text": "Which phase of flight has the greatest occurrence of fatal accidents?",
    "options": [
      "Takeoff.",
      "Landing.",
      "Approach."
    ],
    "correctIndex": 2,
    "explanation": "The pilot’s work requirements vary depending on the mode of flight. The tasks increase significantly during the landing phase, creating greater risk to the pilot and warranting actions that require greater precision and attention, but the greatest percentage of fatal accidents occurs during the approach phase of flight. (PLT509, AA.VI.F.R9) — FAA-H-8083-2",
    "cite": "ASA Airline Transport Pilot Test Prep 2025–2026 · Chapter 7 Emergencies, Hazards, and Flight Physiology · p. 7-10 · question 9858 · ALL",
    "sourceQuestionId": "9858",
    "sourceEdition": "2025–2026",
    "sourcePage": 10,
    "sourceCategories": [
      "ALL"
    ],
    "status": "publicada",
    "source": "import",
    "imagenes": []
  },
  {
    "id": "q_la_ATP_2026_9354",
    "fuente": "ATP",
    "capitulo": 7,
    "capituloTitulo": "Emergencies, Hazards, and Flight Physiology",
    "seccion": "Flight Physiology",
    "materia": "operaciones",
    "text": "Under 14 CFR Part 91, what are the minimum number of hours that must pass after a person consumes alcohol before they may act as a crewmember?",
    "options": [
      "8.",
      "10.",
      "12."
    ],
    "correctIndex": 0,
    "explanation": "No person may act or attempt to act as a crewmember of a civil aircraft within 8 hours after the consumption of any alcoholic beverage. (PLT463, AA.I.F.K2) — 14 CFR §91.17",
    "cite": "ASA Airline Transport Pilot Test Prep 2025–2026 · Chapter 7 Emergencies, Hazards, and Flight Physiology · p. 7-12 · question 9354 · ALL",
    "sourceQuestionId": "9354",
    "sourceEdition": "2025–2026",
    "sourcePage": 12,
    "sourceCategories": [
      "ALL"
    ],
    "status": "publicada",
    "source": "import",
    "imagenes": []
  },
  {
    "id": "q_la_ATP_2026_9354_1",
    "fuente": "ATP",
    "capitulo": 7,
    "capituloTitulo": "Emergencies, Hazards, and Flight Physiology",
    "seccion": "Flight Physiology",
    "materia": "operaciones",
    "text": "A pilot should be aware the alcohol in one beer can be detected for as long as",
    "options": [
      "minimum of 60 minutes.",
      "2 hours.",
      "3 hours."
    ],
    "correctIndex": 2,
    "explanation": "As little as one ounce of liquor, one bottle of beer or four ounces of wine can impair flying skills, with the alcohol consumed in these drinks being detectable in the breath and blood for at least 3 hours. (PLT104, AA.I.F.K2) — AIM ¶8-1-1",
    "cite": "ASA Airline Transport Pilot Test Prep 2025–2026 · Chapter 7 Emergencies, Hazards, and Flight Physiology · p. 7-12 · question 9354-1 · ALL",
    "sourceQuestionId": "9354-1",
    "sourceEdition": "2025–2026",
    "sourcePage": 12,
    "sourceCategories": [
      "ALL"
    ],
    "status": "publicada",
    "source": "import",
    "imagenes": []
  },
  {
    "id": "q_la_ATP_2026_9111",
    "fuente": "ATP",
    "capitulo": 7,
    "capituloTitulo": "Emergencies, Hazards, and Flight Physiology",
    "seccion": "Flight Physiology",
    "materia": "operaciones",
    "text": "What is the effect of alcohol consumption on functions of the body?",
    "options": [
      "Alcohol has an adverse effect, especially as altitude increases.",
      "Small amounts of alcohol in the human system increase judgment and decision-making abilities.",
      "Alcohol has little effect if followed by an ounce of black coffee for every ounce of alcohol."
    ],
    "correctIndex": 0,
    "explanation": "The adverse effect of alcohol is greatly multiplied when a person is exposed to altitude. Two drinks on the ground are equivalent to three or four at altitude. (PLT205, AA.I.F.K2) — AIM ¶8-1-1 Answer (B) is incorrect because even small amounts of alcohol impair judgment and decision-making abilities. Answer (C) is incorrect because there is no way to increase the body’s metabolism of alcohol or to alleviate a hangover (including drinking black coffee).",
    "cite": "ASA Airline Transport Pilot Test Prep 2025–2026 · Chapter 7 Emergencies, Hazards, and Flight Physiology · p. 7-12 · question 9111 · ALL",
    "sourceQuestionId": "9111",
    "sourceEdition": "2025–2026",
    "sourcePage": 12,
    "sourceCategories": [
      "ALL"
    ],
    "status": "publicada",
    "source": "import",
    "imagenes": []
  },
  {
    "id": "q_la_ATP_2026_9111_1",
    "fuente": "ATP",
    "capitulo": 7,
    "capituloTitulo": "Emergencies, Hazards, and Flight Physiology",
    "seccion": "Flight Physiology",
    "materia": "operaciones",
    "text": "Pilot performance can be seriously degraded by",
    "options": [
      "prescribed and over-the-counter medications.",
      "prescription medications only.",
      "over-the-counter medications only."
    ],
    "correctIndex": 0,
    "explanation": "Pilot performance can be seriously degraded by both prescribed and over-the-counter medications, as well as by the medical conditions for which they are taken. Many medications have primary effects that may impair judgment, memory, alertness, coordination, vision, and the ability to make calculations. Others have side effects that may impair the same critical functions. Any medication that depresses the nervous system, such as a sedative, tranquilizer or antihistamine, can make a pilot much more susceptible to hypoxia. Pilots are prohibited from performing crewmember duties while using any medication that affects the faculties in any way contrary to safety. (PLT354, AA.I.F.K2) — AIM ¶8-1-1",
    "cite": "ASA Airline Transport Pilot Test Prep 2025–2026 · Chapter 7 Emergencies, Hazards, and Flight Physiology · p. 7-12 · question 9111-1 · ALL",
    "sourceQuestionId": "9111-1",
    "sourceEdition": "2025–2026",
    "sourcePage": 12,
    "sourceCategories": [
      "ALL"
    ],
    "status": "publicada",
    "source": "import",
    "imagenes": []
  },
  {
    "id": "q_la_ATP_2026_9111_2",
    "fuente": "ATP",
    "capitulo": 7,
    "capituloTitulo": "Emergencies, Hazards, and Flight Physiology",
    "seccion": "Flight Physiology",
    "materia": "operaciones",
    "text": "While experiencing a hangover, a pilot",
    "options": [
      "will have impaired motor and mental responses.",
      "is no longer under the influence of alcohol.",
      "may experience discomfort, but no impairment."
    ],
    "correctIndex": 0,
    "explanation": "While experiencing a hangover, a pilot is still under the influence of alcohol. Although a pilot may think he or she is functioning normally, motor and mental response impairment is still present. Alcohol can remain in the body for over 16 hours, so pilots should be cautious about flying too soon after drinking. (PLT205, AA.I.F.K2) — AIM ¶8-1-1",
    "cite": "ASA Airline Transport Pilot Test Prep 2025–2026 · Chapter 7 Emergencies, Hazards, and Flight Physiology · p. 7-12 · question 9111-2 · ALL",
    "sourceQuestionId": "9111-2",
    "sourceEdition": "2025–2026",
    "sourcePage": 12,
    "sourceCategories": [
      "ALL"
    ],
    "status": "publicada",
    "source": "import",
    "imagenes": []
  },
  {
    "id": "q_la_ATP_2026_9111_3",
    "fuente": "ATP",
    "capitulo": 7,
    "capituloTitulo": "Emergencies, Hazards, and Flight Physiology",
    "seccion": "Flight Physiology",
    "materia": "operaciones",
    "text": "Consumption of alcohol",
    "options": [
      "can severely impair a person for more than 8 hours.",
      "is of no concern in aviation after 8 hours regardless of amount consumed.",
      "in small amounts has no effect on judgment and decision-making."
    ],
    "correctIndex": 0,
    "explanation": "The CFRs prohibit pilots from performing crewmember duties within 8 hours after drinking any alcoholic beverages or while under the influence of alcohol. However, due to the slow metabolism of alcohol, a pilot may still be under the influence 8 hours after a moderate amount of alcohol. Therefore, allow at least 12 to 24 hours between “bottle and throttle” depending on the amount of alcoholic beverage consumed. (PLT205, AA.I.F.K2) — AIM ¶8-1-1",
    "cite": "ASA Airline Transport Pilot Test Prep 2025–2026 · Chapter 7 Emergencies, Hazards, and Flight Physiology · p. 7-12 · question 9111-3 · ALL",
    "sourceQuestionId": "9111-3",
    "sourceEdition": "2025–2026",
    "sourcePage": 12,
    "sourceCategories": [
      "ALL"
    ],
    "status": "publicada",
    "source": "import",
    "imagenes": []
  },
  {
    "id": "q_la_ATP_2026_9107",
    "fuente": "ATP",
    "capitulo": 7,
    "capituloTitulo": "Emergencies, Hazards, and Flight Physiology",
    "seccion": "Flight Physiology",
    "materia": "operaciones",
    "text": "When making an approach to a narrower-thanusual runway, without VASI assistance, the pilot should be aware that the approach",
    "options": [
      "altitude may be higher than it appears.",
      "altitude may be lower than it appears.",
      "may result in leveling off too high and landing hard."
    ],
    "correctIndex": 1,
    "explanation": "An approach to a narrower-than-usual runway can create the illusion that the aircraft is higher than it actually is. (PLT280, AA.I.F.K1k) — AIM ¶8-1-5 Answer (A) is incorrect because wider-than-usual runways may result in higher than desired approaches. Answer (C) is incorrect because leveling off too high is not affected by the runway width, but rather by the pilot’s landing proficiency.",
    "cite": "ASA Airline Transport Pilot Test Prep 2025–2026 · Chapter 7 Emergencies, Hazards, and Flight Physiology · p. 7-13 · question 9107 · ALL",
    "sourceQuestionId": "9107",
    "sourceEdition": "2025–2026",
    "sourcePage": 13,
    "sourceCategories": [
      "ALL"
    ],
    "status": "publicada",
    "source": "import",
    "imagenes": []
  },
  {
    "id": "q_la_ATP_2026_9107_1",
    "fuente": "ATP",
    "capitulo": 7,
    "capituloTitulo": "Emergencies, Hazards, and Flight Physiology",
    "seccion": "Flight Physiology",
    "materia": "operaciones",
    "text": "You have just touched down hard in the rain on a narrower-than-usual runway at night. You realize you have just experienced",
    "options": [
      "runway length illusion.",
      "an atmospheric height illusion.",
      "ground lighting illusion."
    ],
    "correctIndex": 1,
    "explanation": "An approach to a narrower-than-usual runway can create the illusion that the aircraft is higher than it actually is. (PLT280, AA.I.F.K1k) — AIM ¶8-1-5",
    "cite": "ASA Airline Transport Pilot Test Prep 2025–2026 · Chapter 7 Emergencies, Hazards, and Flight Physiology · p. 7-13 · question 9107-1 · ALL",
    "sourceQuestionId": "9107-1",
    "sourceEdition": "2025–2026",
    "sourcePage": 13,
    "sourceCategories": [
      "ALL"
    ],
    "status": "publicada",
    "source": "import",
    "imagenes": []
  },
  {
    "id": "q_la_ATP_2026_9107_2",
    "fuente": "ATP",
    "capitulo": 7,
    "capituloTitulo": "Emergencies, Hazards, and Flight Physiology",
    "seccion": "Flight Physiology",
    "materia": "operaciones",
    "text": "The illusion associated with landing on a narrower than usual runway may result in the pilot flying a",
    "options": [
      "lower approach with the risk of striking objects along the approach path or landing short.",
      "slower approach with the risk of reducing airspeed below VS0 or landing hard.",
      "higher approach with the risk of leveling out high and landing hard or overshooting the runway."
    ],
    "correctIndex": 0,
    "explanation": "An approach to a narrower-than-usual runway can create the illusion that the aircraft is higher than it actually is. (PLT280, AA.I.F.K1k) — AIM ¶8-1-5",
    "cite": "ASA Airline Transport Pilot Test Prep 2025–2026 · Chapter 7 Emergencies, Hazards, and Flight Physiology · p. 7-13 · question 9107-2 · ALL",
    "sourceQuestionId": "9107-2",
