  {
    "id": "q_la_ATP_2026_9109",
    "fuente": "ATP",
    "capitulo": 7,
    "capituloTitulo": "Emergencies, Hazards, and Flight Physiology",
    "seccion": "Flight Physiology",
    "materia": "operaciones",
    "text": "In the dark, a stationary light will appear to move when stared at for a period of time. This illusion is known as",
    "options": [
      "somatogravic illusion.",
      "ground lighting illusion.",
      "autokinesis."
    ],
    "correctIndex": 2,
    "explanation": "In the dark, a stationary light will appear to move about when stared at for many seconds. This illusion is known as Autokinesis. (PLT280, AA.I.F.K1k) — AIM ¶8-1-5 Answer (A) is incorrect because somatogravic illusion occurs with a rapid acceleration during takeoff, creating the illusion of being in a nose-up attitude. Answer (B) is incorrect because a ground lighting illusion refers to lights on a straight path such as a road being mistaken by a pilot for runway or approach lights.",
    "cite": "ASA Airline Transport Pilot Test Prep 2025–2026 · Chapter 7 Emergencies, Hazards, and Flight Physiology · p. 7-13 · question 9109 · ALL",
    "sourceQuestionId": "9109",
    "sourceEdition": "2025–2026",
    "sourcePage": 13,
    "sourceCategories": [
      "ALL"
    ],
    "status": "publicada",
    "source": "import",
    "imagenes": []
  },
  {
    "id": "q_la_ATP_2026_9110",
    "fuente": "ATP",
    "capitulo": 7,
    "capituloTitulo": "Emergencies, Hazards, and Flight Physiology",
    "seccion": "Flight Physiology",
    "materia": "operaciones",
    "text": "When making a landing over darkened or featureless terrain such as water or snow, a pilot should be aware of the possibility of illusion. The approach may appear to be too",
    "options": [
      "high.",
      "low.",
      "shallow."
    ],
    "correctIndex": 0,
    "explanation": "An absence of ground features, when landing over water, darkened areas and terrain made featureless by snow, can create the illusion that the aircraft is at a higher altitude than it actually is. (PLT280, AA.I.F.K1k) — AIM ¶8-1-5",
    "cite": "ASA Airline Transport Pilot Test Prep 2025–2026 · Chapter 7 Emergencies, Hazards, and Flight Physiology · p. 7-13 · question 9110 · ALL",
    "sourceQuestionId": "9110",
    "sourceEdition": "2025–2026",
    "sourcePage": 13,
    "sourceCategories": [
      "ALL"
    ],
    "status": "publicada",
    "source": "import",
    "imagenes": []
  },
  {
    "id": "q_la_ATP_2026_9110_1",
    "fuente": "ATP",
    "capitulo": 7,
    "capituloTitulo": "Emergencies, Hazards, and Flight Physiology",
    "seccion": "Flight Physiology",
    "materia": "operaciones",
    "text": "You have just touched down on the runway faster than planned in the haze at night. You realize that you have just experienced",
    "options": [
      "an atmospheric distance illusion.",
      "a gravotopic height illusion.",
      "the elevator illusion."
    ],
    "correctIndex": 0,
    "explanation": "An absence of ground features, when landing over water, darkened areas and terrain made featureless by snow, can create the illusion that the aircraft is at a higher altitude than it actually is. (PLT280, AA.I.F.K1k) — AIM ¶8-1-5",
    "cite": "ASA Airline Transport Pilot Test Prep 2025–2026 · Chapter 7 Emergencies, Hazards, and Flight Physiology · p. 7-13 · question 9110-1 · ALL",
    "sourceQuestionId": "9110-1",
    "sourceEdition": "2025–2026",
