  {
    "id": "q_la_ATP_2026_9101",
    "fuente": "ATP",
    "capitulo": 7,
    "capituloTitulo": "Emergencies, Hazards, and Flight Physiology",
    "seccion": "Flight Physiology",
    "materia": "operaciones",
    "text": "What is a symptom of carbon monoxide poisoning?",
    "options": [
      "Rapid, shallow breathing.",
      "Pain and cramping of the hands and feet.",
      "Dizziness."
    ],
    "correctIndex": 2,
    "explanation": "Carbon monoxide poisoning produces the same symptoms as hypoxia, which include dizziness. (PLT097, AA.I.F.K1f) — AIM ¶8-1-4 Answer (A) is incorrect because rapid breathing can result in hyperventilation, but it is not a symptom of carbon monoxide poisoning. Answer (B) is incorrect because tingling in the extremities (not pain and cramping) is one symptom of hyperventilation (not carbon monoxide poisoning).",
    "cite": "ASA Airline Transport Pilot Test Prep 2025–2026 · Chapter 7 Emergencies, Hazards, and Flight Physiology · p. 7-16 · question 9101 · ALL",
    "sourceQuestionId": "9101",
    "sourceEdition": "2025–2026",
