    "imagenes": []
  },
  {
    "id": "q_la_ATP_2026_9103",
    "fuente": "ATP",
    "capitulo": 7,
    "capituloTitulo": "Emergencies, Hazards, and Flight Physiology",
    "seccion": "Flight Physiology",
    "materia": "operaciones",
    "text": "Altitude-induced hypoxia is caused by what atmospheric condition?",
    "options": [
      "Significantly less oxygen molecules at high altitude.",
      "Insufficient partial pressure of the inhaled oxygen.",
      "Incorrect balance of oxygen and carbon dioxide."
    ],
    "correctIndex": 1,
    "explanation": "Low partial pressure of oxygen causes hypoxia. (PLT330, AA.I.F.K1a) — AIM ¶8-1-2",
    "cite": "ASA Airline Transport Pilot Test Prep 2025–2026 · Chapter 7 Emergencies, Hazards, and Flight Physiology · p. 7-16 · question 9103 · ALL",
    "sourceQuestionId": "9103",
    "sourceEdition": "2025–2026",
    "sourcePage": 16,
    "sourceCategories": [
      "ALL"
    ],
    "status": "publicada",
    "source": "import",
    "imagenes": []
  },
  {
    "id": "q_la_ATP_2026_9104",
    "fuente": "ATP",
    "capitulo": 7,
    "capituloTitulo": "Emergencies, Hazards, and Flight Physiology",
    "seccion": "Flight Physiology",
    "materia": "operaciones",
    "text": "Which is a common symptom of hyperventilation?",
    "options": [
      "Tingling sensations.",
      "Visual acuity.",
      "Decreased breathing rate."
    ],
    "correctIndex": 0,
    "explanation": "Symptoms of hyperventilation include dizziness, tingling of the extremities, sensation of body heat, rapid heart rate, blurring of vision, muscle spasm, and, finally, unconsciousness. (PLT332, AA.I.F.K1b) — AIM ¶8-1-3 Answer (B) is incorrect because hyperventilation distorts one’s abilities. Answer (C) is incorrect because decreasing the breathing rate overcomes hyperventilation and is not a symptom of it.",
    "cite": "ASA Airline Transport Pilot Test Prep 2025–2026 · Chapter 7 Emergencies, Hazards, and Flight Physiology · p. 7-16 · question 9104 · ALL",
    "sourceQuestionId": "9104",
    "sourceEdition": "2025–2026",
    "sourcePage": 16,
    "sourceCategories": [
      "ALL"
    ],
    "status": "publicada",
    "source": "import",
    "imagenes": []
  },
  {
    "id": "q_la_ATP_2026_9105",
    "fuente": "ATP",
    "capitulo": 7,
    "capituloTitulo": "Emergencies, Hazards, and Flight Physiology",
    "seccion": "Flight Physiology",
    "materia": "operaciones",
    "text": "Loss of cabin pressure may result in hypoxia because as cabin altitude increases",
    "options": [
      "the percentage of nitrogen in the air is increased.",
      "the percentage of oxygen in the air is decreased.",
      "oxygen partial pressure is decreased."
    ],
    "correctIndex": 2,
    "explanation": "Low partial pressure of oxygen causes hypoxia. (PLT330, AA.I.F.K1a) — AIM ¶8-1-2 Answers (A) and (B) are incorrect because the percentage of nitrogen, carbon dioxide and oxygen in the atmosphere remains constant with changes in altitude, but there is less pressure as you increase in altitude.",
    "cite": "ASA Airline Transport Pilot Test Prep 2025–2026 · Chapter 7 Emergencies, Hazards, and Flight Physiology · p. 7-16 · question 9105 · ALL",
    "sourceQuestionId": "9105",
    "sourceEdition": "2025–2026",
    "sourcePage": 16,
    "sourceCategories": [
      "ALL"
    ],
    "status": "publicada",
    "source": "import",
    "imagenes": []
  },
  {
    "id": "q_la_ATP_2026_9106",
    "fuente": "ATP",
    "capitulo": 7,
    "capituloTitulo": "Emergencies, Hazards, and Flight Physiology",
    "seccion": "Flight Physiology",
    "materia": "operaciones",
    "text": "Hypoxia is the result of which of these conditions?",
    "options": [
      "Insufficient oxygen reaching the brain.",
      "Excessive carbon dioxide in the bloodstream.",
      "Limited oxygen reaching the heart muscles."
    ],
    "correctIndex": 0,
    "explanation": "Hypoxia is a result of too little oxygen reaching the brain. (PLT330, AA.I.F.K1a) — AIM ¶8-1-2 Answer (B) is incorrect because excessive carbon dioxide in the blood stream causes hyperventilation. Answer (C) is incorrect because it is the result of insufficient oxygen to the brain.",
    "cite": "ASA Airline Transport Pilot Test Prep 2025–2026 · Chapter 7 Emergencies, Hazards, and Flight Physiology · p. 7-16 · question 9106 · ALL",
    "sourceQuestionId": "9106",
    "sourceEdition": "2025–2026",
    "sourcePage": 16,
    "sourceCategories": [
      "ALL"
    ],
    "status": "publicada",
    "source": "import",
    "imagenes": []
  },
  {
    "id": "q_la_ATP_2026_9856",
    "fuente": "ATP",
    "capitulo": 7,
    "capituloTitulo": "Emergencies, Hazards, and Flight Physiology",
    "seccion": "Flight Physiology",
    "materia": "operaciones",
    "text": "Human behavior",
    "options": [
      "rarely results in accidents unless deliberate actions are performed.",
      "is responsible for three out of four accidents.",
      "is well understood, so behavioral induced accidents are exceedingly rare occurrences."
    ],
    "correctIndex": 1,
    "explanation": "Three out of four accidents result from improper human performance. The human element is the most flexible, adaptable, and valuable part of the aviation system, but it is also the most vulnerable to influences that can adversely affect its performance. (PLT104, AA.I.F.K3) — FAA-H-8083-2",
    "cite": "ASA Airline Transport Pilot Test Prep 2025–2026 · Chapter 7 Emergencies, Hazards, and Flight Physiology · p. 7-16 · question 9856 · ALL",
    "sourceQuestionId": "9856",
    "sourceEdition": "2025–2026",
    "sourcePage": 16,
    "sourceCategories": [
      "ALL"
    ],
    "status": "publicada",
    "source": "import",
    "imagenes": []
  },
  {
    "id": "q_la_ATP_2026_9778",
    "fuente": "ATP",
    "capitulo": 7,
    "capituloTitulo": "Emergencies, Hazards, and Flight Physiology",
    "seccion": "Flight Physiology",
    "materia": "operaciones",
    "text": "An experienced pilot trying to meet a schedule",
    "options": [
      "can expect the flight crew to alert them to problems or areas of concern.",
      "will always err on the side of caution.",
      "can fail to perceive operational pitfalls."
    ],
    "correctIndex": 2,
    "explanation": "Although more experienced pilots are likely to make more automatic decisions, there are tendencies or operational pitfalls that come with the development of pilot experience. These are classic behavioral traps into which pilots have been known to fall. More experienced pilots (as a rule) try to complete a flight as planned, please passengers, and meet schedules. The desire to meet these goals can have an adverse effect on safety and contribute to an unrealistic assessment of piloting skills. (PLT104, AA.I.F.K3) — FAA-H-8083-25",
    "cite": "ASA Airline Transport Pilot Test Prep 2025–2026 · Chapter 7 Emergencies, Hazards, and Flight Physiology · p. 7-17 · question 9778 · ALL",
    "sourceQuestionId": "9778",
    "sourceEdition": "2025–2026",
    "sourcePage": 17,
    "sourceCategories": [
      "ALL"
    ],
    "status": "publicada",
    "source": "import",
    "imagenes": []
  },
  {
    "id": "q_la_ATP_2026_9778_1",
    "fuente": "ATP",
    "capitulo": 7,
    "capituloTitulo": "Emergencies, Hazards, and Flight Physiology",
    "seccion": "Flight Physiology",
    "materia": "operaciones",
    "text": "Automatic Decision-Making is",
    "options": [
      "a reflexive type of decision-making.",
      "an impulsive type of decision-making.",
      "an internalized type of decision-making."
    ],
    "correctIndex": 0,
    "explanation": "Automatic decision making is a reflexive type of decision making anchored in training and experience and is most often used in times of emergency when there is no time to practice analytical decision making. (PLT104, AA.I.F.K3) — FAA-H-8083-2",
    "cite": "ASA Airline Transport Pilot Test Prep 2025–2026 · Chapter 7 Emergencies, Hazards, and Flight Physiology · p. 7-17 · question 9778-1 · ALL",
    "sourceQuestionId": "9778-1",
    "sourceEdition": "2025–2026",
    "sourcePage": 17,
    "sourceCategories": [
      "ALL"
    ],
    "status": "publicada",
    "source": "import",
    "imagenes": []
  },
  {
    "id": "q_la_ATP_2026_9804",
    "fuente": "ATP",
    "capitulo": 7,
    "capituloTitulo": "Emergencies, Hazards, and Flight Physiology",
    "seccion": "Flight Physiology",
    "materia": "operaciones",
    "text": "The crew monitoring function is essential,",
    "options": [
      "particularly during high altitude cruise flight modes to prevent CAT issues.",
      "particularly during approach and landing to prevent CFIT.",
      "during RNAV departures in class B airspace."
    ],
    "correctIndex": 1,
    "explanation": "Effective monitoring and cross-checking can be the last line of defense that prevents an accident because detecting an error or unsafe situation may break the chain of events leading to an accident. This monitoring function is always essential, and particularly so during approach and landing when controlled flight into terrain (CFIT) accidents are most common. (PLT104, AA.I.F.K3) — AC 120-71A",
    "cite": "ASA Airline Transport Pilot Test Prep 2025–2026 · Chapter 7 Emergencies, Hazards, and Flight Physiology · p. 7-17 · question 9804 · ALL",
    "sourceQuestionId": "9804",
    "sourceEdition": "2025–2026",
    "sourcePage": 17,
    "sourceCategories": [
      "ALL"
    ],
    "status": "publicada",
    "source": "import",
    "imagenes": []
  },
  {
    "id": "q_la_ATP_2026_9804_1",
    "fuente": "ATP",
    "capitulo": 7,
    "capituloTitulo": "Emergencies, Hazards, and Flight Physiology",
    "seccion": "Flight Physiology",
    "materia": "operaciones",
    "text": "In order to achieve the highest level of safety,",
    "options": [
      "each flight crewmember must carefully monitor the aircraft’s flight path.",
      "the crewmembers must continually monitor their seat dependent tasks.",
      "the captain’s judgment must not be questioned."
    ],
    "correctIndex": 0,
    "explanation": "The FAA expects the flight crew to monitor the aircraft’s flight path. (PLT354, AA.I.F.K3) — AC 120-71A",
    "cite": "ASA Airline Transport Pilot Test Prep 2025–2026 · Chapter 7 Emergencies, Hazards, and Flight Physiology · p. 7-17 · question 9804-1 · ALL",
    "sourceQuestionId": "9804-1",
    "sourceEdition": "2025–2026",
    "sourcePage": 17,
    "sourceCategories": [
