  },
  {
    "id": "q_la_ATP_2026_9805",
    "fuente": "ATP",
    "capitulo": 7,
    "capituloTitulo": "Emergencies, Hazards, and Flight Physiology",
    "seccion": "Flight Physiology",
    "materia": "operaciones",
    "text": "CRM training refers to",
    "options": [
      "the two components of flight safety and resource management, combined with mentor feedback.",
      "the three components of initial indoctrination awareness, recurrent practice and feedback, and continual reinforcement.",
      "the five components of initial indoctrination awareness, communication principles, recurrent practice and feedback, coordination drills, and continual reinforcement."
    ],
    "correctIndex": 1,
    "explanation": "The critical components of effective crew resource management (CRM) training include initial indoctrination awareness, recurrent practice and feedback, and continual reinforcement. (PLT104, AA.I.F.K3) — AC120-51",
    "cite": "ASA Airline Transport Pilot Test Prep 2025–2026 · Chapter 7 Emergencies, Hazards, and Flight Physiology · p. 7-17 · question 9805 · ALL",
    "sourceQuestionId": "9805",
    "sourceEdition": "2025–2026",
    "sourcePage": 17,
    "sourceCategories": [
      "ALL"
    ],
    "status": "publicada",
    "source": "import",
    "imagenes": []
  },
  {
    "id": "q_la_ATP_2026_9805_1",
    "fuente": "ATP",
    "capitulo": 7,
    "capituloTitulo": "Emergencies, Hazards, and Flight Physiology",
    "seccion": "Flight Physiology",
    "materia": "operaciones",
    "text": "CRM error management includes",
    "options": [
      "effective use of all available resources: human resources, hardware, and information.",
      "error callout and error guidance training.",
      "error prevention, error detection, and recovery from the error."
    ],
    "correctIndex": 2,
    "explanation": "It is now understood that pilot errors cannot be entirely eliminated. It is important, therefore, that pilots develop appropriate error management skills and procedures. It is certainly desirable to prevent as many errors as possible, but since they cannot all be prevented, detection and recovery from errors should be addressed in training. Evaluation of pilots should also consider error management (error prevention, detection, and recovery). (PLT104, AA.I.F.K3) — AC 120-51",
    "cite": "ASA Airline Transport Pilot Test Prep 2025–2026 · Chapter 7 Emergencies, Hazards, and Flight Physiology · p. 7-18 · question 9805-1 · ALL",
    "sourceQuestionId": "9805-1",
    "sourceEdition": "2025–2026",
    "sourcePage": 18,
    "sourceCategories": [
      "ALL"
    ],
    "status": "publicada",
    "source": "import",
    "imagenes": []
  },
  {
    "id": "q_la_ATP_2026_9805_2",
    "fuente": "ATP",
    "capitulo": 7,
    "capituloTitulo": "Emergencies, Hazards, and Flight Physiology",
    "seccion": "Flight Physiology",
    "materia": "operaciones",
    "text": "CRM is defined as",
    "options": [
      "application of team management in the flight deck environment.",
      "the use of human factors principles in the aviation environment.",
      "a human error avoidance approach to aviation management in the flight deck."
    ],
    "correctIndex": 0,
    "explanation": "Crew resource management (CRM) is the application of team management concepts in the flight deck environment. It was initially known as cockpit resource management, but as CRM programs evolved to include cabin crews, maintenance personnel, and others, the phrase “crew resource management” was adopted. (PLT104, AA.I.F.K3) — FAA-H-8083-2",
    "cite": "ASA Airline Transport Pilot Test Prep 2025–2026 · Chapter 7 Emergencies, Hazards, and Flight Physiology · p. 7-18 · question 9805-2 · ALL",
    "sourceQuestionId": "9805-2",
    "sourceEdition": "2025–2026",
    "sourcePage": 18,
    "sourceCategories": [
      "ALL"
    ],
    "status": "publicada",
    "source": "import",
    "imagenes": []
  },
  {
    "id": "q_la_ATP_2026_9805_3",
    "fuente": "ATP",
    "capitulo": 7,
    "capituloTitulo": "Emergencies, Hazards, and Flight Physiology",
    "seccion": "Flight Physiology",
    "materia": "operaciones",
    "text": "Cultural issues in CRM",
    "options": [
      "are not appropriate subjects for effective CRM training in any population.",
      "must be addressed for each training population.",
      "can be discussed if the training syllabus allows training time for those topics."
    ],
    "correctIndex": 1,
    "explanation": "While individuals and even teams of individuals may perform well under many conditions, they are subject to the influence of at least three cultures: the professional cultures of the individuals themselves, the cultures of their organizations, and the national cultures surrounding the individuals and their organizations. If not recognized and addressed, factors related to culture may degrade crew performance. Hence, effective CRM training must address culture issues, as appropriate in each training population. (PLT104, AA.I.F.K3) — AC 120-51",
    "cite": "ASA Airline Transport Pilot Test Prep 2025–2026 · Chapter 7 Emergencies, Hazards, and Flight Physiology · p. 7-18 · question 9805-3 · ALL",
    "sourceQuestionId": "9805-3",
    "sourceEdition": "2025–2026",
    "sourcePage": 18,
    "sourceCategories": [
      "ALL"
    ],
    "status": "publicada",
    "source": "import",
    "imagenes": []
  },
  {
    "id": "q_la_ATP_2026_9805_4",
    "fuente": "ATP",
    "capitulo": 7,
    "capituloTitulo": "Emergencies, Hazards, and Flight Physiology",
    "seccion": "Flight Physiology",
    "materia": "operaciones",
    "text": "Crew resource management (CRM) is",
    "options": [
      "a dilution of the captain’s authority.",
      "the only method of accident avoidance and error prevention.",
      "one way to make good decisions."
    ],
    "correctIndex": 2,
    "explanation": "CRM refers to the effective use of all available resources: human resources, hardware, and information. CRM training is one way of addressing the challenge of optimizing the human/machine interface and accompanying interpersonal activities, including decision making. (PLT104, AA.I.F.K3) — AC 120-51",
    "cite": "ASA Airline Transport Pilot Test Prep 2025–2026 · Chapter 7 Emergencies, Hazards, and Flight Physiology · p. 7-18 · question 9805-4 · ALL",
    "sourceQuestionId": "9805-4",
    "sourceEdition": "2025–2026",
    "sourcePage": 18,
    "sourceCategories": [
      "ALL"
    ],
    "status": "publicada",
    "source": "import",
    "imagenes": []
  },
  {
    "id": "q_la_ATP_2026_9805_5",
    "fuente": "ATP",
    "capitulo": 7,
    "capituloTitulo": "Emergencies, Hazards, and Flight Physiology",
    "seccion": "Flight Physiology",
    "materia": "operaciones",
    "text": "Risk management, as part of the aeronautical decision making (ADM) process, relies on which features to reduce the risks associated with each flight?",
    "options": [
      "Application of stress management and risk element procedures.",
      "Situational awareness, problem recognition, and good judgment.",
      "The mental process of analyzing all information in a particular situation and making a timely decision on what action to take."
    ],
    "correctIndex": 1,
    "explanation": "Risk management is the part of the decision making process which relies on situational awareness, problem recognition, and good judgment to reduce risks associated with each flight. (PLT104, AA.I.F.K3) — FAA-H-8083-2",
    "cite": "ASA Airline Transport Pilot Test Prep 2025–2026 · Chapter 7 Emergencies, Hazards, and Flight Physiology · p. 7-18 · question 9805-5 · ALL",
    "sourceQuestionId": "9805-5",
    "sourceEdition": "2025–2026",
    "sourcePage": 18,
    "sourceCategories": [
      "ALL"
    ],
    "status": "publicada",
    "source": "import",
    "imagenes": []
  },
  {
    "id": "q_la_ATP_2026_9805_6",
    "fuente": "ATP",
    "capitulo": 7,
    "capituloTitulo": "Emergencies, Hazards, and Flight Physiology",
    "seccion": "Flight Physiology",
    "materia": "operaciones",
    "text": "In order to assess risk in aeronautical decision making, what two basic considerations are recommended?",
    "options": [
      "Convenience and effort required.",
      "Likelihood and severity.",
      "Time and cost efficiency."
    ],
    "correctIndex": 1,
    "explanation": "The degree of risk posed by a given hazard can be measured in terms of exposure (number of people or resources affected), severity (extent of possible loss), and probability (the likelihood that a hazard will cause a loss). (PLT104, AA.I.F.K3) — FAA-H-8083-25",
    "cite": "ASA Airline Transport Pilot Test Prep 2025–2026 · Chapter 7 Emergencies, Hazards, and Flight Physiology · p. 7-18 · question 9805-6 · ALL",
    "sourceQuestionId": "9805-6",
    "sourceEdition": "2025–2026",
    "sourcePage": 18,
    "sourceCategories": [
      "ALL"
    ],
    "status": "publicada",
    "source": "import",
    "imagenes": []
  },
  {
    "id": "q_la_ATP_2026_9805_7",
    "fuente": "ATP",
    "capitulo": 7,
    "capituloTitulo": "Emergencies, Hazards, and Flight Physiology",
    "seccion": "Flight Physiology",
    "materia": "operaciones",
    "text": "To improve the effectiveness and safety of the entire operations team as a working system, CRM training should include",
    "options": [
      "usage of seat-dependent checklists.",
      "employee groups beyond the flight crew.",
      "failures the flight crew must work through as a team."
    ],
    "correctIndex": 2,
    "explanation": "CRM is the application of team management concepts in the flight deck environment. CRM is one way of addressing the challenge of optimizing the human/machine interface and accompanying interpersonal activities. (PLT104, AA.I.E.K12) — FAA-H-8083-25",
    "cite": "ASA Airline Transport Pilot Test Prep 2025–2026 · Chapter 7 Emergencies, Hazards, and Flight Physiology · p. 7-19 · question 9805-7 · ALL",
    "sourceQuestionId": "9805-7",
    "sourceEdition": "2025–2026",
    "sourcePage": 19,
    "sourceCategories": [
      "ALL"
    ],
    "status": "publicada",
