    "imagenes": []
  },
  {
    "id": "q_la_ATP_2026_9940",
    "fuente": "ATP",
    "capitulo": 7,
    "capituloTitulo": "Emergencies, Hazards, and Flight Physiology",
    "seccion": "Flight Physiology",
    "materia": "operaciones",
    "text": "One purpose of crew resource management (CRM) is to give crews the tools to",
    "options": [
      "recognize and mitigate hazards.",
      "maintain currency with regulations.",
      "reduce the need for outside resources."
    ],
    "correctIndex": 0,
    "explanation": "CRM training is one way of addressing the challenge of optimizing the human/machine interface and accompanying interpersonal activities, including recognizing and mitigating hazards. (PLT104, AA.I.F.K3) — AC 120-51",
    "cite": "ASA Airline Transport Pilot Test Prep 2025–2026 · Chapter 7 Emergencies, Hazards, and Flight Physiology · p. 7-19 · question 9940 · ALL",
    "sourceQuestionId": "9940",
    "sourceEdition": "2025–2026",
    "sourcePage": 19,
    "sourceCategories": [
      "ALL"
    ],
    "status": "publicada",
    "source": "import",
    "imagenes": []
  },
  {
    "id": "q_la_ATP_2026_9806",
    "fuente": "ATP",
    "capitulo": 7,
    "capituloTitulo": "Emergencies, Hazards, and Flight Physiology",
    "seccion": "Flight Physiology",
    "materia": "operaciones",
    "text": "Error management evaluation",
    "options": [
      "should recognize not all errors can be prevented.",
      "may include error evaluation that should have been prevented.",
      "must mark errors as disqualifying."
    ],
    "correctIndex": 0,
    "explanation": "It is certainly desirable to prevent as many errors as possible, but since they cannot all be prevented, detection and recovery from errors should be addressed in training. Error management (error prevention, detection, and recovery) should be considered in the evaluation of pilots, as well as the fact that since not all errors can be prevented, it is important that errors be managed properly. (PLT104, AA.I.F.K3) — AC120-51",
    "cite": "ASA Airline Transport Pilot Test Prep 2025–2026 · Chapter 7 Emergencies, Hazards, and Flight Physiology · p. 7-19 · question 9806 · ALL",
    "sourceQuestionId": "9806",
    "sourceEdition": "2025–2026",
    "sourcePage": 19,
    "sourceCategories": [
      "ALL"
    ],
    "status": "publicada",
    "source": "import",
    "imagenes": []
  },
  {
    "id": "q_la_ATP_2026_9806_1",
    "fuente": "ATP",
    "capitulo": 7,
    "capituloTitulo": "Emergencies, Hazards, and Flight Physiology",
    "seccion": "Flight Physiology",
    "materia": "operaciones",
    "text": "The most important key to risk management is",
    "options": [
      "understanding pilot predisposition.",
      "management of external pressures.",
      "the sense of security provided by experience."
    ],
    "correctIndex": 1,
    "explanation": "Management of external pressure is the single most important key to risk management because it is the one risk factor category that can cause a pilot to ignore all the other risk factors. External pressures put time-related pressure on the pilot and figure into a majority of accidents. (PLT271, AA.I.F.K3) — FAA-H-8083-25",
    "cite": "ASA Airline Transport Pilot Test Prep 2025–2026 · Chapter 7 Emergencies, Hazards, and Flight Physiology · p. 7-19 · question 9806-1 · ALL",
    "sourceQuestionId": "9806-1",
    "sourceEdition": "2025–2026",
    "sourcePage": 19,
    "sourceCategories": [
      "ALL"
    ],
    "status": "publicada",
    "source": "import",
    "imagenes": []
  },
  {
    "id": "q_la_ATP_2026_9816",
    "fuente": "ATP",
    "capitulo": 7,
    "capituloTitulo": "Emergencies, Hazards, and Flight Physiology",
    "seccion": "Flight Physiology",
    "materia": "operaciones",
    "text": "An experienced pilot mistakes the runway heading for the instructed heading for departure. What kind of error is this?",
    "options": [
      "Experience error.",
      "Detection error.",
      "Insight detection."
    ],
    "correctIndex": 1,
    "explanation": "Problem detection is the first step in the decisionmaking process. It begins with recognizing that a change occurred or that an expected change did not occur. A problem is perceived first by the senses and then it is determined through insight and experience. These same abilities, as well as an objective analysis of all available information, are used to determine the nature and severity of the problem. Therefore, incorrectly detecting the problem to begin with is an error that is critical during a decision-making process. (PLT104, AA.I.F.K3) — FAA-H-8083-25A",
    "cite": "ASA Airline Transport Pilot Test Prep 2025–2026 · Chapter 7 Emergencies, Hazards, and Flight Physiology · p. 7-19 · question 9816 · ALL",
    "sourceQuestionId": "9816",
    "sourceEdition": "2025–2026",
    "sourcePage": 19,
    "sourceCategories": [
      "ALL"
    ],
    "status": "publicada",
    "source": "import",
    "imagenes": []
  },
  {
    "id": "q_la_ATP_2026_9816_1",
    "fuente": "ATP",
    "capitulo": 7,
    "capituloTitulo": "Emergencies, Hazards, and Flight Physiology",
    "seccion": "Flight Physiology",
    "materia": "operaciones",
    "text": "An experienced, current, and proficient pilot fails to notice the landing gear is not extended for landing. This is an example of",
    "options": [
      "systems training.",
      "problem detection.",
      "procedures knowledge."
    ],
    "correctIndex": 1,
    "explanation": "Problem detection is the first step in the decisionmaking process. It begins with recognizing that a change occurred or that an expected change did not occur. A problem is perceived first by the senses and then it is determined through insight and experience. These same abilities, as well as an objective analysis of all available information, are used to determine the nature and severity of the problem. Therefore, incorrectly detecting a problem to begin with is an error that is critical during a decision-making process. (PLT104, AA.I.F.K3) — FAA-H-8083-25",
    "cite": "ASA Airline Transport Pilot Test Prep 2025–2026 · Chapter 7 Emergencies, Hazards, and Flight Physiology · p. 7-19 · question 9816-1 · ALL",
    "sourceQuestionId": "9816-1",
    "sourceEdition": "2025–2026",
    "sourcePage": 19,
    "sourceCategories": [
      "ALL"
    ],
    "status": "publicada",
    "source": "import",
    "imagenes": []
  },
  {
    "id": "q_la_ATP_2026_9832",
    "fuente": "ATP",
    "capitulo": 7,
    "capituloTitulo": "Emergencies, Hazards, and Flight Physiology",
    "seccion": "Flight Physiology",
    "materia": "operaciones",
    "text": "Accident-prone pilots tend to",
    "options": [
      "have disdain toward rules.",
      "follow methodical information gathering techniques.",
      "excessively utilize outside resources."
    ],
    "correctIndex": 0,
    "explanation": "One of the primary characteristics exhibited by accidentprone pilots was their disdain toward rules. (PLT103, AA.I.F.K3) — FAA-H-8083-2",
    "cite": "ASA Airline Transport Pilot Test Prep 2025–2026 · Chapter 7 Emergencies, Hazards, and Flight Physiology · p. 7-20 · question 9832 · ALL",
    "sourceQuestionId": "9832",
    "sourceEdition": "2025–2026",
    "sourcePage": 20,
    "sourceCategories": [
      "ALL"
    ],
    "status": "publicada",
    "source": "import",
    "imagenes": []
  },
  {
    "id": "q_la_ATP_2026_9815",
    "fuente": "ATP",
    "capitulo": 7,
    "capituloTitulo": "Emergencies, Hazards, and Flight Physiology",
    "seccion": "Flight Physiology",
    "materia": "operaciones",
    "text": "When a recently certificated pilot decides to not wait any longer for the fog and low ceilings to burn off, this pilot may be exhibiting the hazardous",
    "options": [
      "resigned attitude.",
      "macho attitude.",
      "impulsive attitude."
    ],
    "correctIndex": 2,
    "explanation": "ADM addresses the following five hazardous attitudes: anti-authority (don’t tell me!), impulsivity (do something quickly!), invulnerability (it won’t happen to me), macho (I can do it), resignation (what’s the use?). This pilot is exhibiting an impulsive attitude by departing before conditions warrant. (PLT103, AA.I.F.R2) — FAA-H-8083-2",
    "cite": "ASA Airline Transport Pilot Test Prep 2025–2026 · Chapter 7 Emergencies, Hazards, and Flight Physiology · p. 7-20 · question 9815 · ALL",
    "sourceQuestionId": "9815",
    "sourceEdition": "2025–2026",
    "sourcePage": 20,
    "sourceCategories": [
      "ALL"
    ],
    "status": "publicada",
    "source": "import",
    "imagenes": []
  },
  {
    "id": "q_la_ATP_2026_9815_1",
    "fuente": "ATP",
    "capitulo": 7,
    "capituloTitulo": "Emergencies, Hazards, and Flight Physiology",
    "seccion": "Flight Physiology",
    "materia": "operaciones",
    "text": "When an aircraft pilot takes off as ordered in the afternoon when embedded thunderstorms are indicated on radar and NOAA is broadcasting weather watches, it might be described as an example of",
    "options": [
      "the resigned effect.",
      "an anti-authority attitude.",
      "an impulsive attitude."
    ],
    "correctIndex": 0,
    "explanation": "ADM addresses the following five hazardous attitudes: anti-authority (don’t tell me!), impulsivity (do something quickly!), invulnerability (it won’t happen to me), macho (I can do it), resignation (what’s the use?). This pilot is exhibiting a resigned attitude by leaving the departure decision up to others. Pilots who think, “what’s the use?” will leave the action to others and may even go along with unreasonable requests. (PLT103, AA.I.F.R2) — FAA-H-8083-25 Answer (B) is incorrect because the question involves an example of the pilot following the authority to a fault. Answer (C) is incorrect because the question involves an example of a pilot following orders; the impulsive attitude is demonstrated when the pilot does not select the best alternative and they do the first thing that comes to mind.",
    "cite": "ASA Airline Transport Pilot Test Prep 2025–2026 · Chapter 7 Emergencies, Hazards, and Flight Physiology · p. 7-20 · question 9815-1 · ALL",
    "sourceQuestionId": "9815-1",
    "sourceEdition": "2025–2026",
    "sourcePage": 20,
    "sourceCategories": [
      "ALL"
    ],
    "status": "publicada",
    "source": "import",
    "imagenes": []
  },
  {
    "id": "q_la_ATP_2026_9833",
    "fuente": "ATP",
    "capitulo": 7,
    "capituloTitulo": "Emergencies, Hazards, and Flight Physiology",
    "seccion": "Flight Physiology",
    "materia": "operaciones",
    "text": "An air carrier crew fixated on completing the last flight of a four day trip often may exhibit",
    "options": [
      "get-there-itis.",
      "staged decision-making.",
      "naturalistic decision-making."
    ],
    "correctIndex": 0,
    "explanation": "There are a number of classic behavioral traps into which pilots have been known to fall. These dangerous tendencies or behavior patterns, which must be identified and eliminated, include: peer pressure, mind set, get-there-itis, duck-under syndrome, scud running, continuing visual flight rules into instrument conditions, getting behind the aircraft, loss of positional or situation awareness, operating without adequate fuel reserves, descent below the minimum enroute altitude, flying outside the envelope, neglect of flight planning, preflight inspections, checklists, etc. (PLT104, AA.I.F.R2) — FAA-H-8083-2",
    "cite": "ASA Airline Transport Pilot Test Prep 2025–2026 · Chapter 7 Emergencies, Hazards, and Flight Physiology · p. 7-20 · question 9833 · ALL",
    "sourceQuestionId": "9833",
    "sourceEdition": "2025–2026",
    "sourcePage": 20,
    "sourceCategories": [
      "ALL"
    ],
    "status": "publicada",
    "source": "import",
    "imagenes": []
  },
  {
    "id": "q_la_ATP_2026_9834",
    "fuente": "ATP",
    "capitulo": 7,
    "capituloTitulo": "Emergencies, Hazards, and Flight Physiology",
    "seccion": "Flight Physiology",
    "materia": "operaciones",
    "text": "An air carrier aircraft flown into the ground while troubleshooting a landing gear fault is an example of",
    "options": [
      "neglect and reliance on memory.",
      "loss of situational awareness.",
      "lack of aviation experience."
    ],
    "correctIndex": 1,
    "explanation": "Instances of human factor accidents include operational errors that relate to loss of situational awareness and flying outside the envelope. These can be termed as operational pitfalls. (PLT104, AA.I.F.K3) — FAA-H8083-2",
    "cite": "ASA Airline Transport Pilot Test Prep 2025–2026 · Chapter 7 Emergencies, Hazards, and Flight Physiology · p. 7-20 · question 9834 · ALL",
    "sourceQuestionId": "9834",
    "sourceEdition": "2025–2026",
    "sourcePage": 20,
    "sourceCategories": [
      "ALL"
    ],
    "status": "publicada",
    "source": "import",
    "imagenes": []
  },
  {
    "id": "q_la_ATP_2026_9927",
    "fuente": "ATP",
    "capitulo": 7,
    "capituloTitulo": "Emergencies, Hazards, and Flight Physiology",
    "seccion": "Flight Physiology",
    "materia": "operaciones",
    "text": "A pilot making a blood donation in order to help a sick associate should be aware that for several weeks",
    "options": [
      "sufficient oxygen may not reach the cells in the body.",
      "fewer oxygen molecules will be available to the respiratory membranes.",
      "the ability of the body tissues to effectively use oxygen is decreased."
    ],
    "correctIndex": 0,
    "explanation": "Hypemic hypoxia occurs when the blood is not able to take up and transport a sufficient amount of oxygen to the cells in the body. Hypemic hypoxia can be caused by the loss of blood due to blood donation. Blood volume can require several weeks to return to normal following a donation. Although the effects of the blood loss are slight at ground level, there are risks when flying during this time. (PLT330, AA.I.F.K1a) — FAA-H-8083-25",
    "cite": "ASA Airline Transport Pilot Test Prep 2025–2026 · Chapter 7 Emergencies, Hazards, and Flight Physiology · p. 7-20 · question 9927 · ALL",
    "sourceQuestionId": "9927",
    "sourceEdition": "2025–2026",
    "sourcePage": 20,
    "sourceCategories": [
      "ALL"
    ],
    "status": "publicada",
    "source": "import",
    "imagenes": []
  },
  {
    "id": "q_la_ATP_2026_9928",
    "fuente": "ATP",
    "capitulo": 7,
    "capituloTitulo": "Emergencies, Hazards, and Flight Physiology",
    "seccion": "Flight Physiology",
    "materia": "operaciones",
    "text": "Stress distraction can interfere with judgment to the extent that",
    "options": [
      "unwarranted risks are taken.",
      "physical response rates to stimuli are impaired.",
      "perceptions are clouded."
    ],
    "correctIndex": 0,
    "explanation": "Stress causes concentration and performance problems. (PLT098, AA.I.F.K1g) — FAA-H-8083-25",
    "cite": "ASA Airline Transport Pilot Test Prep 2025–2026 · Chapter 7 Emergencies, Hazards, and Flight Physiology · p. 7-21 · question 9928 · ALL",
    "sourceQuestionId": "9928",
    "sourceEdition": "2025–2026",
    "sourcePage": 21,
    "sourceCategories": [
      "ALL"
    ],
    "status": "publicada",
    "source": "import",
    "imagenes": []
  },
  {
    "id": "q_la_ATP_2026_9928_1",
    "fuente": "ATP",
    "capitulo": 7,
    "capituloTitulo": "Emergencies, Hazards, and Flight Physiology",
    "seccion": "Flight Physiology",
    "materia": "operaciones",
    "text": "What type of stressor can lead to poor decision making?",
    "options": [
      "Lack of sleep.",
      "Lack of high workload.",
      "Lack of motivation."
    ],
    "correctIndex": 0,
    "explanation": "Fatigue is frequently associated with pilot error. Some of the effects of fatigue include degradation of attention and concentration, impaired coordination, and decreased ability to communicate. These factors seriously influence the ability to make effective decisions. (PLT098, AA.I.F.K3) — FAA-H-8083-25",
    "cite": "ASA Airline Transport Pilot Test Prep 2025–2026 · Chapter 7 Emergencies, Hazards, and Flight Physiology · p. 7-21 · question 9928-1 · ALL",
    "sourceQuestionId": "9928-1",
    "sourceEdition": "2025–2026",
    "sourcePage": 21,
    "sourceCategories": [
      "ALL"
    ],
    "status": "publicada",
    "source": "import",
    "imagenes": []
  },
  {
    "id": "q_la_ATP_2026_9929",
    "fuente": "ATP",
    "capitulo": 7,
    "capituloTitulo": "Emergencies, Hazards, and Flight Physiology",
    "seccion": "Flight Physiology",
    "materia": "operaciones",
    "text": "Sleep inertia refers to a period of",
    "options": [
      "heightened alertness and visual acuity following a rest period.",
      "alignment between a person’s internal biological clock and local external time cues.",
      "impaired performance following awakening from a regular sleep cycle or nap."
    ],
    "correctIndex": 2,
    "explanation": "Sleep inertia (also termed sleep drunkenness) refers to a period of impaired performance and reduced vigilance following awakening from the regular sleep episode or nap. This impairment may be severe, lasting from minutes to hours, and be accompanied by micro-sleep episodes. (PLT104, AA.I.F.K1h) — AC 120-100",
    "cite": "ASA Airline Transport Pilot Test Prep 2025–2026 · Chapter 7 Emergencies, Hazards, and Flight Physiology · p. 7-21 · question 9929 · ALL",
    "sourceQuestionId": "9929",
    "sourceEdition": "2025–2026",
    "sourcePage": 21,
    "sourceCategories": [
      "ALL"
    ],
    "status": "publicada",
    "source": "import",
    "imagenes": []
  },
  {
    "id": "q_la_ATP_2026_9929_1",
    "fuente": "ATP",
    "capitulo": 7,
    "capituloTitulo": "Emergencies, Hazards, and Flight Physiology",
    "seccion": "Flight Physiology",
    "materia": "operaciones",
    "text": "The maximum tailwind component of the airplane is 10 knots. The actual tailwind calculated is 11 knots. Other aircraft are continuing to land, so you decide to ignore the limitation and land as well. Which hazardous attitude are you displaying?",
    "options": [
      "Impulsivity.",
      "Resignation.",
      "Anti-authority."
    ],
    "correctIndex": 2,
    "explanation": "The anti-authority attitude is demonstrated by someone ignoring the rules. (PLT104, AA.I.F.R2) — FAA-H-8083-2 Answer (A) is incorrect because impulsivity is the attitude of people who frequently feel the need to do something, anything, immediately. Answer (B) is incorrect because resignation attitude is demonstrated by someone thinking “what’s the use.”",
    "cite": "ASA Airline Transport Pilot Test Prep 2025–2026 · Chapter 7 Emergencies, Hazards, and Flight Physiology · p. 7-21 · question 9929-1 · ALL",
    "sourceQuestionId": "9929-1",
    "sourceEdition": "2025–2026",
    "sourcePage": 21,
    "sourceCategories": [
      "ALL"
    ],
    "status": "publicada",
    "source": "import",
    "imagenes": []
  },
  {
    "id": "q_la_ATP_2026_9929_2",
    "fuente": "ATP",
    "capitulo": 7,
    "capituloTitulo": "Emergencies, Hazards, and Flight Physiology",
    "seccion": "Flight Physiology",
    "materia": "operaciones",
    "text": "Which of the following is one of the five traits discovered to be common in pilots who have had accidents in their past?",
    "options": [
      "A low correlation between traffic safety violations and flying safety mishaps.",
      "A tendency to be impulsive rather than disciplined, especially in decision making.",
      "A sense of respect for rules and procedures."
    ],
    "correctIndex": 1,
    "explanation": "The five traits discovered in pilots prone to having accidents are: (1) disdain toward rules; (2) very high correlation between accidents on their flying records and safety violations on their driving records; (3) frequently fall into the “thrill and adventure seeking” personality category; (4) impulsive rather than methodical and disciplined, both in their information gathering and in the speed and selection of actions to be taken; (5) a disregard for or tend to underutilize outside sources of information, including copilots, flight attendants, flight service personnel, flight instructors, and ATC. (PLT104, AA.I.F.K3) — FAA-H-8083-25",
    "cite": "ASA Airline Transport Pilot Test Prep 2025–2026 · Chapter 7 Emergencies, Hazards, and Flight Physiology · p. 7-21 · question 9929-2 · ALL",
    "sourceQuestionId": "9929-2",
    "sourceEdition": "2025–2026",
    "sourcePage": 21,
    "sourceCategories": [
      "ALL"
    ],
    "status": "publicada",
    "source": "import",
    "imagenes": []
  },
  {
    "id": "q_la_ATP_2026_9152",
    "fuente": "ATP",
    "capitulo": 8,
    "capituloTitulo": "Meteorology and Weather Services",
    "seccion": "The Atmosphere",
    "materia": "operaciones",
    "text": "What is the primary cause of all changes in the Earth’s weather?",
    "options": [
      "Variations of solar energy at the Earth’s surface.",
      "Changes in air pressure over the Earth’s surface.",
      "Movement of air masses from moist areas to dry areas."
    ],
    "correctIndex": 0,
    "explanation": "Every physical process of weather is accompanied by or is the result of a heat exchange. Differences in solar energy create temperature variations. These temperature variations create forces that drive the atmosphere in its endless motion. (PLT510, AA.I.C.K3c) — FAA-H8083-28 Answer (B) is incorrect because changes in air pressure are due to temperature variations. Answer (C) is incorrect because movement of air masses is a result of varying temperatures and pressures.",
    "cite": "ASA Airline Transport Pilot Test Prep 2025–2026 · Chapter 8 Meteorology and Weather Services · p. 8-4 · question 9152 · ALL",
    "sourceQuestionId": "9152",
    "sourceEdition": "2025–2026",
    "sourcePage": 4,
    "sourceCategories": [
      "ALL"
    ],
    "status": "publicada",
    "source": "import",
    "imagenes": []
  },
  {
    "id": "q_la_ATP_2026_9160",
    "fuente": "ATP",
    "capitulo": 8,
    "capituloTitulo": "Meteorology and Weather Services",
    "seccion": "The Atmosphere",
    "materia": "operaciones",
    "text": "Where is the usual location of a thermal low?",
    "options": [
      "Over the arctic region.",
      "Over the eye of a hurricane.",
      "Over the surface of a dry, sunny region."
    ],
    "correctIndex": 2,
    "explanation": "A dry, sunny region can become quite warm from intense surface heating, thus generating a surface low-pressure area. This is called a thermal low. (PLT511, AA.I.C.K3e) — FAA-H-8083-28 Answer (A) is incorrect because thermal lows develop over dry, sunny regions, not in the arctic. Answer (B) is incorrect because the eye of a hurricane marks the center of a well-developed tropical cyclone.",
    "cite": "ASA Airline Transport Pilot Test Prep 2025–2026 · Chapter 8 Meteorology and Weather Services · p. 8-5 · question 9160 · ALL",
    "sourceQuestionId": "9160",
    "sourceEdition": "2025–2026",
    "sourcePage": 5,
    "sourceCategories": [
      "ALL"
    ],
    "status": "publicada",
    "source": "import",
    "imagenes": []
  },
  {
    "id": "q_la_ATP_2026_9159",
    "fuente": "ATP",
    "capitulo": 8,
    "capituloTitulo": "Meteorology and Weather Services",
    "seccion": "The Atmosphere",
    "materia": "operaciones",
    "text": "What is a feature of air movement in a high pressure area?",
    "options": [
      "Ascending from the surface high to lower pressure at higher altitudes.",
      "Descending to the surface and then outward.",
      "Moving outward from the high at high altitudes and into the high at the surface."
    ],
    "correctIndex": 1,
    "explanation": "Air in a high pressure system (in the northern hemisphere) tends to descend to the surface and then spiral out from the center of the high. (PLT173, AA.I.C.K3e) — FAA-H-8083-28",
    "cite": "ASA Airline Transport Pilot Test Prep 2025–2026 · Chapter 8 Meteorology and Weather Services · p. 8-5 · question 9159 · ALL",
    "sourceQuestionId": "9159",
    "sourceEdition": "2025–2026",
    "sourcePage": 5,
    "sourceCategories": [
      "ALL"
    ],
    "status": "publicada",
    "source": "import",
    "imagenes": []
  },
  {
    "id": "q_la_ATP_2026_9157",
    "fuente": "ATP",
    "capitulo": 8,
    "capituloTitulo": "Meteorology and Weather Services",
    "seccion": "The Atmosphere",
    "materia": "operaciones",
    "text": "At lower levels of the atmosphere, friction causes the wind to flow across isobars into a low because the friction",
    "options": [
      "decreases windspeed and Coriolis force.",
      "decreases pressure gradient force.",
      "creates air turbulence and raises atmospheric pressure."
    ],
    "correctIndex": 0,
    "explanation": "Frictional force slows wind speed near the surface and Coriolis force is decreased. The stronger pressure gradient force turns the wind at an angle across the isobars toward lower pressure until the three forces (Coriolis, pressure gradient, and friction) are in balance. (PLT173, AA.I.C.K3e) — FAA-H-8083-28 Answer (B) is incorrect because friction does not affect the pressure gradient force. Answer (C) is incorrect because of surface obstructions.",
    "cite": "ASA Airline Transport Pilot Test Prep 2025–2026 · Chapter 8 Meteorology and Weather Services · p. 8-5 · question 9157 · ALL",
    "sourceQuestionId": "9157",
    "sourceEdition": "2025–2026",
    "sourcePage": 5,
    "sourceCategories": [
      "ALL"
    ],
    "status": "publicada",
    "source": "import",
    "imagenes": []
  },
  {
    "id": "q_la_ATP_2026_9176",
    "fuente": "ATP",
    "capitulo": 8,
    "capituloTitulo": "Meteorology and Weather Services",
    "seccion": "The Atmosphere",
    "materia": "operaciones",
    "text": "At which location does Coriolis force have the least effect on wind direction?",
    "options": [
      "At the poles.",
      "Middle latitudes (30° to 60°).",
      "At the equator."
    ],
    "correctIndex": 2,
    "explanation": "Coriolis force varies with latitude from zero at the equator to a maximum at the poles. (PLT510, AA.I.C.K3e) — FAA-H-8083-28",
    "cite": "ASA Airline Transport Pilot Test Prep 2025–2026 · Chapter 8 Meteorology and Weather Services · p. 8-5 · question 9176 · ALL",
    "sourceQuestionId": "9176",
    "sourceEdition": "2025–2026",
    "sourcePage": 5,
    "sourceCategories": [
      "ALL"
    ],
    "status": "publicada",
    "source": "import",
    "imagenes": []
  },
  {
    "id": "q_la_ATP_2026_9177",
    "fuente": "ATP",
    "capitulo": 8,
    "capituloTitulo": "Meteorology and Weather Services",
    "seccion": "The Atmosphere",
    "materia": "operaciones",
    "text": "How does Coriolis force affect wind direction in the Southern Hemisphere?",
    "options": [
      "Causes clockwise rotation around a low.",
      "Causes wind to flow out of a low toward a high.",
      "Has exactly the same effect as in the Northern Hemisphere."
    ],
    "correctIndex": 0,
    "explanation": "The Coriolis force deflects air flow to the right causing winds above the friction level to flow parallel to the isobars. In the northern hemisphere, winds flow clockwise around high-pressure areas and counterclockwise around low-pressure areas. In the southern hemisphere, Coriolis force causes a counterclockwise flow around highs and a clockwise flow around lows. (PLT516, AA.I.C.K3e) — FAA-H-8083-28 Answer (B) is incorrect because the wind flows from a high to a low (not a low to a high) in both the northern and southern hemispheres. Answer (C) is incorrect because the Coriolis force deflects air to the left in the southern hemisphere, which is the opposite effect from the northern hemisphere.",
    "cite": "ASA Airline Transport Pilot Test Prep 2025–2026 · Chapter 8 Meteorology and Weather Services · p. 8-5 · question 9177 · ALL",
    "sourceQuestionId": "9177",
    "sourceEdition": "2025–2026",
    "sourcePage": 5,
    "sourceCategories": [
      "ALL"
    ],
    "status": "publicada",
    "source": "import",
    "imagenes": []
  },
  {
    "id": "q_la_ATP_2026_9178",
    "fuente": "ATP",
    "capitulo": 8,
    "capituloTitulo": "Meteorology and Weather Services",
    "seccion": "The Atmosphere",
    "materia": "operaciones",
    "text": "Which weather condition is defined as an anti-cyclone?",
    "options": [
      "Calm.",
      "High pressure area.",
      "Col."
    ],
    "correctIndex": 1,
    "explanation": "The clockwise flow of air around a high-pressure area in the northern hemisphere is called an anticyclone. (PLT173, AA.I.C.K3e) — FAA-H-8083-28 Answer (A) is incorrect because calm is defined as the absence of wind or of apparent motion of the air. Answer (C) is incorrect because a col is the neutral area between two highs or two lows. It is also the intersection of a trough and a ridge.",
    "cite": "ASA Airline Transport Pilot Test Prep 2025–2026 · Chapter 8 Meteorology and Weather Services · p. 8-5 · question 9178 · ALL",
    "sourceQuestionId": "9178",
    "sourceEdition": "2025–2026",
    "sourcePage": 5,
    "sourceCategories": [
      "ALL"
    ],
    "status": "publicada",
    "source": "import",
    "imagenes": []
  },
  {
    "id": "q_la_ATP_2026_9178_1",
    "fuente": "ATP",
    "capitulo": 8,
    "capituloTitulo": "Meteorology and Weather Services",
    "seccion": "The Atmosphere",
    "materia": "operaciones",
    "text": "A cyclone is",
    "options": [
      "a hurricane force storm in the Indian Ocean.",
      "a tropical depression with sustained winds of 63 knots.",
      "a tropical depression with a barometric pressure in the center of the feature that is 35 mb lower than pressure outside the weather feature."
    ],
    "correctIndex": 0,
    "explanation": "The low pressure and its wind system is a cyclone. Strong tropical cyclones are known by different names in different regions of the world. A tropical cyclone in the Atlantic and eastern Pacific is a “hurricane”; in the western Pacific, “typhoon”; and in the Indian Ocean, simply “cyclone.” (PLT517, AA.I.C.K3e) — FAA-H-8083-28",
    "cite": "ASA Airline Transport Pilot Test Prep 2025–2026 · Chapter 8 Meteorology and Weather Services · p. 8-6 · question 9178-1 · ALL",
    "sourceQuestionId": "9178-1",
    "sourceEdition": "2025–2026",
    "sourcePage": 6,
    "sourceCategories": [
      "ALL"
    ],
    "status": "publicada",
    "source": "import",
    "imagenes": []
  },
  {
    "id": "q_la_ATP_2026_9156",
    "fuente": "ATP",
    "capitulo": 8,
    "capituloTitulo": "Meteorology and Weather Services",
