  {
    "id": "q_la_ATP_2026_8710",
    "fuente": "ATP",
    "capitulo": 8,
    "capituloTitulo": "Meteorology and Weather Services",
    "seccion": "The Atmosphere",
    "materia": "operaciones",
    "text": "For a flight to an airport in the vicinity of the coast, land surface cooling means you can expect to encounter",
    "options": [
      "sea breezes.",
      "land breezes.",
      "a chinook wind."
    ],
    "correctIndex": 1,
    "explanation": "During the day, the sun heats up both the ocean surface and the land. However, water heats up much more slowly than land and so the air above the land will be warmer compared to the air over the ocean. The warm air over the land will rise throughout the day, causing low pressure at the surface. Over the water, high surface pressure will form because of the colder air. To compensate, the air will sink over the ocean. The wind will blow from the higher pressure over the water to lower pressure over the land causing the sea breeze. The opposite occurs at night, with land surface cooling causing a land breeze. (PLT512, AA.I.C.K3e) — FAA-H-8083-28 Answer (A) is incorrect because a sea breeze is cause by land surface warming. Answer (C) is incorrect because a chinook wind is associated with mountainous terrain.",
    "cite": "ASA Airline Transport Pilot Test Prep 2025–2026 · Chapter 8 Meteorology and Weather Services · p. 8-8 · question 8710 · ALL",
    "sourceQuestionId": "8710",
    "sourceEdition": "2025–2026",
    "sourcePage": 8,
    "sourceCategories": [
      "ALL"
    ],
    "status": "publicada",
    "source": "import",
    "imagenes": []
  },
  {
    "id": "q_la_ATP_2026_9165",
    "fuente": "ATP",
    "capitulo": 8,
    "capituloTitulo": "Meteorology and Weather Services",
    "seccion": "Weather Systems",
    "materia": "operaciones",
    "text": "What term describes an elongated area of low pressure?",
    "options": [
      "Trough.",
      "Ridge.",
      "Hurricane or typhoon."
    ],
    "correctIndex": 0,
    "explanation": "A trough is an elongated area of low pressure with the lowest pressure along a line marking maximum anticyclonic curvature. (PLT173, AA.I.C.K3e) — FAA-H-8083-28 Answer (B) is incorrect because a ridge is an elongated area of high pressure. Answer (C) is incorrect because a hurricane or typhoon is a tropical cyclone (low) with highest sustained winds of 65 knots or greater.",
    "cite": "ASA Airline Transport Pilot Test Prep 2025–2026 · Chapter 8 Meteorology and Weather Services · p. 8-9 · question 9165 · ALL",
    "sourceQuestionId": "9165",
    "sourceEdition": "2025–2026",
    "sourcePage": 9,
    "sourceCategories": [
      "ALL"
    ],
    "status": "publicada",
    "source": "import",
    "imagenes": []
  },
  {
    "id": "q_la_ATP_2026_9165_1",
    "fuente": "ATP",
    "capitulo": 8,
    "capituloTitulo": "Meteorology and Weather Services",
    "seccion": "Weather Systems",
    "materia": "operaciones",
    "text": "Low pressure areas are areas of",
    "options": [
      "stagnant air.",
      "descending air.",
      "ascending air."
    ],
    "correctIndex": 2,
    "explanation": "At the surface when air converges into a low, it cannot go outward against the pressure gradient, nor can it go downward into the ground; it must go upward. Therefore, a low or trough is an area of rising air. (PLT173, AA.I.C.K3e) — FAA-H-8083-28",
    "cite": "ASA Airline Transport Pilot Test Prep 2025–2026 · Chapter 8 Meteorology and Weather Services · p. 8-9 · question 9165-1 · ALL",
    "sourceQuestionId": "9165-1",
    "sourceEdition": "2025–2026",
    "sourcePage": 9,
    "sourceCategories": [
      "ALL"
    ],
    "status": "publicada",
    "source": "import",
    "imagenes": []
  },
  {
    "id": "q_la_ATP_2026_9191",
    "fuente": "ATP",
    "capitulo": 8,
    "capituloTitulo": "Meteorology and Weather Services",
    "seccion": "Weather Systems",
    "materia": "operaciones",
    "text": "What is a feature of a stationary front?",
    "options": [
      "The warm front surface moves about half the speed of the cold front surface.",
      "Weather conditions are a combination of strong cold front and strong warm front weather.",
      "Surface winds tend to flow parallel to the frontal zone."
    ],
    "correctIndex": 2,
    "explanation": "The opposing forces exerted by adjacent air masses in a stationary front are such that the frontal surface between them shows little or no movement. In such cases, the surface winds tend to blow parallel to the frontal zone. (PLT511, AA.I.C.K3e) — FAA-H-8083-28 Answer (A) is incorrect because the movement of a warm front surface in comparison to a cold front surface has nothing to do with a stationary front. Answer (B) is incorrect because weather conditions that are a combination of strong cold front and strong warm front weather are a feature of an occluded front.",
    "cite": "ASA Airline Transport Pilot Test Prep 2025–2026 · Chapter 8 Meteorology and Weather Services · p. 8-9 · question 9191 · ALL",
    "sourceQuestionId": "9191",
    "sourceEdition": "2025–2026",
    "sourcePage": 9,
    "sourceCategories": [
      "ALL"
    ],
    "status": "publicada",
    "source": "import",
    "imagenes": []
  },
  {
    "id": "q_la_ATP_2026_9192",
    "fuente": "ATP",
    "capitulo": 8,
    "capituloTitulo": "Meteorology and Weather Services",
    "seccion": "Weather Systems",
    "materia": "operaciones",
    "text": "Which event usually occurs after an aircraft passes through a front into the colder air?",
    "options": [
      "Temperature/dewpoint spread decreases.",
      "Wind direction shifts to the left.",
      "Atmospheric pressure increases."
    ],
    "correctIndex": 2,
    "explanation": "A front lies in a pressure trough, and pressure generally is higher in the cold air. Thus, when crossing a front directly into colder air, the pressure will usually rise abruptly. (PLT511, AA.I.C.K3e) — FAA-H-8083-28 Answer (A) is incorrect because the temperature/dew point spread usually differs across a front. But it might not decrease if you fly in to a cold, dry air mass. Answer (B) is incorrect because in the northern hemisphere the wind always shifts to the right due to the Coriolis force.",
    "cite": "ASA Airline Transport Pilot Test Prep 2025–2026 · Chapter 8 Meteorology and Weather Services · p. 8-9 · question 9192 · ALL",
    "sourceQuestionId": "9192",
    "sourceEdition": "2025–2026",
    "sourcePage": 9,
    "sourceCategories": [
      "ALL"
    ],
    "status": "publicada",
    "source": "import",
    "imagenes": []
  },
  {
    "id": "q_la_ATP_2026_9213",
    "fuente": "ATP",
    "capitulo": 8,
    "capituloTitulo": "Meteorology and Weather Services",
    "seccion": "Weather Systems",
    "materia": "operaciones",
    "text": "If the winds aloft are blowing parallel to the front,",
    "options": [
      "the front can be expected to move with the upper winds.",
      "the winds aloft can be expected to turn at the frontal boundary.",
      "the front moves slowly if at all."
    ],
    "correctIndex": 2,
    "explanation": "The upper winds will dictate to a great extent the movement of the front, along with the amount of cloudiness and rain accompanying the frontal system. If winds aloft parallel a front, the front moves slowly if at all. If winds aloft blow across a front it tends to move with the wind. (PLT511, AA.I.C.K3e) — FAA-H-8083-28",
    "cite": "ASA Airline Transport Pilot Test Prep 2025–2026 · Chapter 8 Meteorology and Weather Services · p. 8-9 · question 9213 · ALL",
    "sourceQuestionId": "9213",
    "sourceEdition": "2025–2026",
    "sourcePage": 9,
    "sourceCategories": [
      "ALL"
    ],
    "status": "publicada",
    "source": "import",
    "imagenes": []
  },
  {
    "id": "q_la_ATP_2026_9215",
    "fuente": "ATP",
    "capitulo": 8,
    "capituloTitulo": "Meteorology and Weather Services",
    "seccion": "Weather Systems",
    "materia": "operaciones",
    "text": "Which atmospheric factor causes rapid movement of surface fronts?",
    "options": [
      "Upper winds blowing across the front.",
      "Upper low located directly over the surface low.",
      "The cold front overtaking and lifting the warm front."
    ],
    "correctIndex": 0,
    "explanation": "Cold fronts move at about the speed of the wind component perpendicular to the front just above the frictional layer. (PLT511, AA.I.C.K3e) — FAA-H-8083-28 Answer (B) is incorrect because an upper low located directly over a surface low would be a factor in how extensive the weather would be, not in how fast it would move. Answer (C) is incorrect because a cold front overtaking and lifting the warm front is a characteristic of an advancing cold front.",
    "cite": "ASA Airline Transport Pilot Test Prep 2025–2026 · Chapter 8 Meteorology and Weather Services · p. 8-9 · question 9215 · ALL",
    "sourceQuestionId": "9215",
    "sourceEdition": "2025–2026",
    "sourcePage": 9,
    "sourceCategories": [
      "ALL"
    ],
    "status": "publicada",
    "source": "import",
    "imagenes": []
  },
  {
    "id": "q_la_ATP_2026_9215_1",
    "fuente": "ATP",
    "capitulo": 8,
    "capituloTitulo": "Meteorology and Weather Services",
    "seccion": "Weather Systems",
    "materia": "operaciones",
    "text": "Dew point fronts result from",
    "options": [
      "air density differences due to the humidity levels.",
      "air density due to temperature.",
      "temperatures aloft."
    ],
    "correctIndex": 0,
    "explanation": "During a considerable part of the year, dew point fronts are common in Western Texas and New Mexico northward over the plains states. Moist air flowing north from the Gulf of Mexico abuts the drier and therefore slightly denser air flowing from the southwest. Except for moisture differences, there is seldom any significant air mass contrast across this front; therefore, it is commonly called a dry line. Nighttime and early morning fog and low level clouds often prevail on the moist side of the line while generally clear skies mark the dry side. (PLT511, AA.I.C.K3e) — FAA-H-8083-28",
    "cite": "ASA Airline Transport Pilot Test Prep 2025–2026 · Chapter 8 Meteorology and Weather Services · p. 8-10 · question 9215-1 · ALL",
    "sourceQuestionId": "9215-1",
    "sourceEdition": "2025–2026",
    "sourcePage": 10,
    "sourceCategories": [
      "ALL"
    ],
    "status": "publicada",
    "source": "import",
    "imagenes": []
  },
  {
    "id": "q_la_ATP_2026_9216",
    "fuente": "ATP",
    "capitulo": 8,
    "capituloTitulo": "Meteorology and Weather Services",
    "seccion": "Weather Systems",
    "materia": "operaciones",
    "text": "In which meteorological conditions can frontal waves and low pressure areas form?",
    "options": [
      "Warm fronts or occluded fronts.",
      "Slow-moving cold fronts or stationary fronts.",
      "Cold front occlusions."
    ],
    "correctIndex": 1,
    "explanation": "Frontal waves and cyclones (areas of low pressure) usually form on slow moving cold fronts or on stationary fronts. (PLT511, AA.I.C.K3e) — FAA-H-8083-28 Answer (A) is incorrect because occluded fronts are formed by frontal waves and areas of low pressure which cause a cold front to close together with a warm front. Frontal waves and low pressure areas normally form on slow-moving cold fronts. Answer (C) is incorrect because a cold front occlusion occurs when the air behind the cold front is colder than the air in advance of the warm front, lifting the warm front aloft.",
    "cite": "ASA Airline Transport Pilot Test Prep 2025–2026 · Chapter 8 Meteorology and Weather Services · p. 8-10 · question 9216 · ALL",
    "sourceQuestionId": "9216",
    "sourceEdition": "2025–2026",
    "sourcePage": 10,
    "sourceCategories": [
      "ALL"
    ],
    "status": "publicada",
    "source": "import",
    "imagenes": []
  },
  {
    "id": "q_la_ATP_2026_9217",
    "fuente": "ATP",
    "capitulo": 8,
    "capituloTitulo": "Meteorology and Weather Services",
    "seccion": "Weather Systems",
    "materia": "operaciones",
    "text": "What weather difference is found on each side of a “dry line”?",
    "options": [
      "Extreme temperature difference.",
      "Dewpoint difference.",
      "Stratus versus cumulus clouds."
    ],
    "correctIndex": 1,
    "explanation": "A dew point front or dry line is formed when two air masses of similar density and temperature meet. Except for the moisture differences, there is little contrast across the front. (PLT511, AA.I.C.K3e) — FAA-H-8083-28 Answer (A) is incorrect because except for moisture (not extreme temperature) difference, there is seldom any significant air mass contrast across the dry line. Answer (C) is incorrect because the side with moisture may have clouds, while generally clear skies mark the dry side.",
    "cite": "ASA Airline Transport Pilot Test Prep 2025–2026 · Chapter 8 Meteorology and Weather Services · p. 8-10 · question 9217 · ALL",
    "sourceQuestionId": "9217",
    "sourceEdition": "2025–2026",
    "sourcePage": 10,
    "sourceCategories": [
