  {
    "id": "q_la_ATP_2026_9194",
    "fuente": "ATP",
    "capitulo": 8,
    "capituloTitulo": "Meteorology and Weather Services",
    "seccion": "Fog and Rain",
    "materia": "operaciones",
    "text": "Which condition produces weather on the lee side of a large lake?",
    "options": [
      "Warm air flowing over a colder lake may produce fog.",
      "Cold air flowing over a warmer lake may produce advection fog.",
      "Warm air flowing over a cool lake may produce rain showers."
    ],
    "correctIndex": 0,
    "explanation": "When warm air flows over a colder lake, the air may become saturated by evaporation from the water while also becoming cooler in the low levels by contact with the cool water. Fog often becomes extensive and dense to the lee (downwind) side of the lake. (PLT226, AA.I.C.K3j) — FAA-H-8083-28 Answer (B) is incorrect because cold air flowing over a warmer lake may produce rain showers, not advection fog, on the lee side of the lake. Answer (C) is incorrect because warm air flowing over a cool lake may produce fog, not rain showers, on the lee side of the lake.",
    "cite": "ASA Airline Transport Pilot Test Prep 2025–2026 · Chapter 8 Meteorology and Weather Services · p. 8-17 · question 9194 · ALL",
