  {
    "id": "q_la_ATP_2026_9204",
    "fuente": "ATP",
    "capitulo": 8,
    "capituloTitulo": "Meteorology and Weather Services",
    "seccion": "Thunderstorms",
    "materia": "operaciones",
    "text": "What is a difference between an air mass thunderstorm and a steady-state thunderstorm?",
    "options": [
      "Air mass thunderstorms produce precipitation which falls outside of the updraft.",
      "Air mass thunderstorm downdrafts and precipitation retard and reverse the updrafts.",
      "Steady-state thunderstorms are associated with local surface heating."
    ],
    "correctIndex": 1,
    "explanation": "Air mass thunderstorms most often result from surface heating. When the storm reaches the mature stage, rain falls through or immediately beside the updraft. Falling precipitation induces frictional drag, retards the updraft and reverses it to a downdraft. Such a self-destructive cell usually has a life cycle of 20 minutes to 1-1/2 hours. In a steady-state thunderstorm, the precipitation falls outside the downdraft and so the cell can last several hours. (PLT495, AA.I.C.K3h) — FAA-H-8083-28 Answer (A) is incorrect because steady-state, not air mass, thunderstorms produce precipitation which falls outside the updraft. Answer (C) is incorrect because air mass, not steady-state, thunderstorms are associated with local surface heating.",
    "cite": "ASA Airline Transport Pilot Test Prep 2025–2026 · Chapter 8 Meteorology and Weather Services · p. 8-19 · question 9204 · ALL",
    "sourceQuestionId": "9204",
    "sourceEdition": "2025–2026",
    "sourcePage": 19,
    "sourceCategories": [
      "ALL"
    ],
    "status": "publicada",
    "source": "import",
    "imagenes": []
  },
  {
    "id": "q_la_ATP_2026_9205",
    "fuente": "ATP",
    "capitulo": 8,
    "capituloTitulo": "Meteorology and Weather Services",
    "seccion": "Thunderstorms",
    "materia": "operaciones",
    "text": "Which type storms are most likely to produce funnel clouds or tornadoes?",
    "options": [
      "Air mass thunderstorms.",
      "Cold front or squall line thunderstorms.",
      "Storms associated with icing and supercooled water."
    ],
    "correctIndex": 1,
    "explanation": "Tornadoes occur with isolated thunderstorms at times, but much more frequently, they form with steady-state thunderstorms associated with cold fronts or squall lines. (PLT495, AA.I.C.K3h) — FAA-H-8083-28 Answer (A) is incorrect because even though air mass thunderstorms may produce funnel clouds or tornadoes, they are most likely to occur with steady-state thunderstorms. Answer (C) is incorrect because all thunderstorms that have updrafts and carry water above the freezing level can produce icing and supercooled water; but thunderstorms associated with cold fronts and squall lines are most likely to produce funnel clouds or tornadoes.",
    "cite": "ASA Airline Transport Pilot Test Prep 2025–2026 · Chapter 8 Meteorology and Weather Services · p. 8-20 · question 9205 · ALL",
    "sourceQuestionId": "9205",
    "sourceEdition": "2025–2026",
