  {
    "id": "q_la_ATP_2026_9220",
    "fuente": "ATP",
    "capitulo": 8,
    "capituloTitulo": "Meteorology and Weather Services",
    "seccion": "Wind Shear",
    "materia": "operaciones",
    "text": "In comparison to an approach in a moderate headwind, which is an indication of a possible wind shear due to a decreasing headwind when descending on the glide slope?",
    "options": [
      "Less power is required.",
      "Higher pitch attitude is required.",
      "Lower descent rate is required."
    ],
    "correctIndex": 1,
    "explanation": "When a headwind shears to calm or a tailwind, the aircraft tends to lose airspeed, get low, and pitch nose down. The aircraft will require more power and a higher pitch attitude to stay on glide slope. (PLT518, AA.I.C.K3b) — AC 00-54 Answer (A) is incorrect because as airspeed decreases, more power is required. Answer (C) is incorrect because as the headwind decreases, ground speed will increase, requiring a higher descent rate.",
    "cite": "ASA Airline Transport Pilot Test Prep 2025–2026 · Chapter 8 Meteorology and Weather Services · p. 8-23 · question 9220 · ALL",
    "sourceQuestionId": "9220",
    "sourceEdition": "2025–2026",
