  {
    "id": "q_la_ATP_2026_9150",
    "fuente": "ATP",
    "capitulo": 8,
    "capituloTitulo": "Meteorology and Weather Services",
    "seccion": "Wind Shear",
    "materia": "operaciones",
    "text": "What is the expected duration of an individual microburst?",
    "options": [
      "Two minutes with maximum winds lasting approximately 1 minute.",
      "One microburst may continue for as long as 2 to 4 hours.",
      "Seldom longer than 15 minutes from the time the burst strikes the ground until dissipation."
    ],
    "correctIndex": 2,
    "explanation": "An individual microburst will seldom last longer than 15 minutes from the time it strikes the ground until dissipation. The horizontal winds continue to increase during the first 5 minutes with the maximum intensity winds lasting approximately 2 to 4 minutes. (PLT317, AA.I.C.K3h) — AIM ¶7-1-24 Answer (A) is incorrect because microbursts last 15, not 2 minutes, and maximum winds last 2 to 4 minutes, not 1 minute. Answer (B) is incorrect because the maximum winds last 2 to 4 minutes, not 2 to 4 hours, and the microburst is usually limited to about 15 minutes.",
    "cite": "ASA Airline Transport Pilot Test Prep 2025–2026 · Chapter 8 Meteorology and Weather Services · p. 8-28 · question 9150 · ALL",
    "sourceQuestionId": "9150",
    "sourceEdition": "2025–2026",
    "sourcePage": 28,
    "sourceCategories": [
      "ALL"
    ],
    "status": "publicada",
    "source": "import",
    "imagenes": []
  },
  {
    "id": "q_la_ATP_2026_9167",
    "fuente": "ATP",
    "capitulo": 8,
    "capituloTitulo": "Meteorology and Weather Services",
    "seccion": "Wind Shear",
    "materia": "operaciones",
    "text": "What information from the control tower is indicated by the following transmission? “SOUTH BOUNDARY WIND ONE SIX ZERO AT TWO FIVE, WEST BOUNDARY WIND TWO FOUR ZERO AT THREE FIVE.”",
    "options": [
      "A downburst is located at the center of the airport.",
      "Wake turbulence exists on the west side of the active runway.",
      "There is a possibility of wind shear over or near the airport."
    ],
    "correctIndex": 2,
    "explanation": "The Low-Level Wind Shear Alert System (LLWAS) is a computerized system which detects the presence of a possible hazardous low-level wind shear by continuously comparing the winds measured by sensors installed around the periphery on an airport with the wind measured at the center field location. If the difference between the center field wind sensor and peripheral sensor becomes excessive, a thunderstorm or thunderstorm gust front wind shear is probable. When this condition exists, the tower controller will provide arrival and departure aircraft with an advisory of the situation which includes the center field wind plus the remote location and wind. The broadcast quoted in the question is an example of this type of advisory. (PLT044, AA.I.C.K2) — AIM ¶4-3-7 Answer (A) is incorrect because a downburst is a vertical movement of air which is not measured by the LLWAS until it has horizontal movement. Also the wind direction is toward the center of the airport, not away from it. Answer (B) is incorrect because wake turbulence does not produce wind. It is generated by an aircraft that is producing lift, which could be on either side of the active runway.",
    "cite": "ASA Airline Transport Pilot Test Prep 2025–2026 · Chapter 8 Meteorology and Weather Services · p. 8-28 · question 9167 · ALL",
    "sourceQuestionId": "9167",
    "sourceEdition": "2025–2026",
    "sourcePage": 28,
    "sourceCategories": [
      "ALL"
    ],
    "status": "publicada",
    "source": "import",
    "imagenes": []
  },
  {
    "id": "q_la_ATP_2026_9136",
    "fuente": "ATP",
    "capitulo": 8,
    "capituloTitulo": "Meteorology and Weather Services",
    "seccion": "Wind Shear",
    "materia": "operaciones",
    "text": "What is the recommended technique to counter the loss of airspeed and resultant lift from wind shear?",
    "options": [
      "Lower the pitch attitude and regain lost airspeed.",
      "Avoid overstressing the aircraft, “pitch to airspeed,” and apply maximum power.",
      "Maintain, or increase, pitch attitude and accept the lower-than-normal airspeed indications."
    ],
    "correctIndex": 2,
    "explanation": "Pitch attitude must be maintained or increased even when lower-than-normal airspeed indications are required. (PLT518, AA.I.C.K3b) — AC 00-54 Answer (A) is incorrect because lowering the pitch attitude to regain lost airspeed is a result of past training emphasis on airspeed control, not recovering from a wind shear. Answer (B) is incorrect because the recommended technique to recover from a wind shear is to maintain or increase pitch attitude and not “pitch to airspeed,” which may decrease pitch to regain lost airspeed.",
    "cite": "ASA Airline Transport Pilot Test Prep 2025–2026 · Chapter 8 Meteorology and Weather Services · p. 8-29 · question 9136 · ALL",
    "sourceQuestionId": "9136",
    "sourceEdition": "2025–2026",
    "sourcePage": 29,
    "sourceCategories": [
      "ALL"
    ],
    "status": "publicada",
    "source": "import",
    "imagenes": []
  },
  {
