  {
    "id": "q_la_ATP_2026_9221",
    "fuente": "ATP",
    "capitulo": 8,
    "capituloTitulo": "Meteorology and Weather Services",
    "seccion": "Frost and Ice",
    "materia": "operaciones",
    "text": "What condition is necessary for the formation of structural icing in flight?",
    "options": [
      "Supercooled water drops.",
      "Water vapor.",
      "Visible water."
    ],
    "correctIndex": 2,
    "explanation": "For structural icing to form, the aircraft must be flying through visible moisture and the temperature where the moisture strikes the aircraft must 0°C or colder. Note that the moisture does not need to be supercooled. (PLT274, AA.I.C.K3i) — FAA-H-8083-28 Answer (A) is incorrect because supercooled water drops increase the rate of icing, but are not a condition necessary for the formation of structural icing. Answer (B) is incorrect because water must be visible, not in a gaseous (vapor) state.",
    "cite": "ASA Airline Transport Pilot Test Prep 2025–2026 · Chapter 8 Meteorology and Weather Services · p. 8-35 · question 9221 · ALL",
