  {
    "id": "q_la_ATP_2026_9162",
    "fuente": "ATP",
    "capitulo": 8,
    "capituloTitulo": "Meteorology and Weather Services",
    "seccion": "Frost and Ice",
    "materia": "operaciones",
    "text": "What temperature condition is indicated if precipitation in the form of wet snow occurs during flight?",
    "options": [
      "The temperature is above freezing at flight altitude.",
      "The temperature is above freezing at higher altitudes.",
      "There is an inversion with colder air below."
    ],
    "correctIndex": 0,
    "explanation": "Snowflakes are formed by sublimation in below-freezing temperatures. If the snow falls into an area of abovefreezing temperatures it will start to melt, become wet snow and eventually turn into rain. (PLT493, AA.I.C.K3d) — FAA-H-8083-28 Answer (B) is incorrect because wet snow indicates above-freezing temperature at flight level, not at higher altitudes. The temperature was below freezing at the altitudes where the snow formed. Answer (C) is incorrect because wet snow indicates falling snow that has begun to melt due to above-freezing temperature at flight level. An inversion may or may not be the cause of the warmer air.",
    "cite": "ASA Airline Transport Pilot Test Prep 2025–2026 · Chapter 8 Meteorology and Weather Services · p. 8-36 · question 9162 · ALL",
